{"raw": {"https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Chief-of-Staff-to-the-President_JR100363": {"title": "Chief of Staff to the President", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 DISCLAIMER: Seattle University is partnering with Isaacson, Miller to conduct the search for this position. To be considered for the opportunity, interested candidates must submit their application materials directly through the Isaacson, Miller application link. Please note: Applications submitted through the Seattle University careers site will not be considered for this search. To apply, please use the link under \"Position Description\" Work Model: Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: To apply please use this link : https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/seattle-university/chief-staff-president?utm_source=chatgpt.com The Chief of Staff will be an integral member of the Office of the President and will bring strong communication, project management, and organizational skills to the team. The COS role covers the following key duties and responsibilities: Act as a trusted advisor, strategic resource, and representative for the President Serve as the President\u2019s aide, trusted advisor, and confidant on matters of strategic and institutional importance. Advise the President on priorities and provide confidential and candid counsel, recommendations, and strategic guidance on matters requiring presidential attention. Surface competing viewpoints, identify institutional implications, and be attentive to potential structural inequities to be resolved. Act as the President\u2019s delegate and representative in leadership meetings, strategic discussions, and other engagements, as assigned, to facilitate executive decision-making, ensure follow-through on presidential directives, and exercise sound judgment to move initiatives forward. Brief the President on key initiatives, projects, meetings, and emerging issues across the university. Prepare and/or contribute to reports, briefings, presentations, communications, and responses on institutional matters as appropriate. Review, research, and synthesize complex information and data to support the President in decision-making. Support the President in addressing faculty, student, parent, alumni, trustee, regent, and public affairs matters and governance, and directly manage issues of institutional importance on the President\u2019s behalf when appropriate. Advance presidential priorities through strategic projects and initiatives Lead and coordinate special projects and strategic initiatives assigned by the President, frequently involving senior leaders across the university to advance institutional priorities. Facilitate collaboration, communication, and follow-through among members of the President\u2019s leadership team and across the institution to advance shared goals and priorities. Monitor and drive progress on priority initiatives, working closely with campus leaders to ensure accountability, remove barriers, resolve challenges, elevate issues requiring executive action, and communicate progress to the campus community as appropriate in support of university goals. Anticipate, identify, and analyze emerging issues, risks, and opportunities that may affect the President\u2019s priorities or the university\u2019s strategic objectives, and recommend courses of action as appropriate. Lead and coordinate the operations and priorities of the Office of the President and the University leadership teams Establish and maintain an integrated view of the President's institutional priorities, commitments, decisions, and emerging issues. Assist in optimizing the President\u2019s schedule by managing competing institutional and community priorities and requests, relieving the President of administrative detail. Oversee communications and correspondence originating from and directed to the President\u2019s office, including reviewing, screening, routing, and drafting responses. Lead the coordination of executive leadership priorities by shaping agendas, facilitating strategic discussions, tracking key decisions and commitments, and ensuring follow-through across the President\u2019s Office, including the President\u2019s Roundtable and Senior Leadership Team, and associated offices. Provide leadership and oversight for the day-to-day operations of the Office of the President, including supervision, mentorship, and evaluation of staff and stewardship of the office budget. Maintain accurate confidential files, records, and institutional information. Maintain a broad understanding of the university\u2019s programs, operations, policies, guidelines, and procedures to support effective leadership and decision-making Support the President\u2019s fundraising and external engagement efforts Partner with University Advancement to support the President\u2019s fundraising, cultivation of donors, and other stewardship activities. Participate in planning and strategy discussions related to donor prospects, fundraising initiatives, and advancement events. Support the President\u2019s engagement with elected officials, civic leaders, business leaders, and other external stakeholders to strengthen strategic relationships and enhance the university\u2019s visibility and impact. Coordinate scheduling priorities and follow-up activities related to donor meetings and engagement efforts. Assist in preparing briefing materials, correspondence, and meeting preparations for donor and external constituent engagements. Serve as the primary liaison resource between the President and the Board of Trustees Serve as staff support and liaison between the President and the Board of Trustees, including the Chair and Executive Committee, on behalf of the President\u2019s Office. Partner with University Counsel and Board Secretary to support Board of Trustee meetings. Assist with planning agendas and overseeing the preparation and distribution of Board materials, meeting notices, and related communications, including preparation and dissemination of monthly reports. Oversee the logistics and administration of Board of Trustees meetings. Collaborate with the Secretary to coordinate trustee onboarding, orientation, and ongoing communication efforts to strengthen engagement with the President\u2019s Office. Compensation at a Glance To view compensation for this position, please follow this link to the Isaacson, Miller job posting. https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/seattle-university/chief-staff-president?utm_source=chatgpt.com Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Digital-Operations---Customer-Experience-Coordinator_JR100082": {"title": "Digital Operations & Customer Experience Coordinator", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Customer Service / Help Desk - 30% Facilities Resource Center point of contact on an as-needed basis for Facilities requests and emergencies via the Call Queue and Work Order Console Respond to inbound inquiries from students, faculty, and staff via Teams, providing clear and professional assistance Maintain accurate records of customer interactions and escalate issues as needed Responsible for the coordination and timely issuance of received information to Facilities staff for completion Prepare and analyze reports on customer engagement metrics CMMS Management \u2013 30% Responsible for the maintenance and operation of the Computerized Maintenance Management System (CMMS) Function as a liaison with the CMMS vendor for day-to-day technical needs Maintain databases to support operational needs Maintain and update the Preventative Maintenance program in the CMMS Generate and analyze reports to support decision-making and compliance Provide training and technical support to community CMMS users Compile CMMS data and update reports for key performance indicators Digital Tools & Technical Support \u2013 25% Supports Facilities and Campus Services software, hardware, and functions: Access Control Key Management System Facilities Software Adobe CS, Bluebeam, DirectLine, Office 365, and others. Websites, internal and external Hardware and software support for Facilities and Campus Services Staff Digital Content Maintenance - 15% Demonstrate a foundational understanding of digital content management with an eagerness to learn and grow in the role Monitor and maintain customer-facing resources Perform content updates for both internal and external websites Maintain company resources, ensuring organization and ease of access Oversee directories to keep information current and well-structured Administer file organization to support efficient data storage and retrieval Marginal Job Functions Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree plus two years of experience in customer service or a combination of education and experience sufficient to perform the essential functions of the position Proficient in MS Office with strong data entry skills Demonstrated agility in rapidly learning and applying new applications to address organizational needs Preferred Qualifications One year of experience using and managing a CMMS Experience in database management Experience in web design or content management Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $55,000 - $60,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Kinesiology-Program-Manager_JR100154": {"title": "Kinesiology Program Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Laboratory Learning Coordination; Human Performance Lab and Mobile Lab (50%) Maintain, update, and produce laboratory operating policies and procedures reflecting industry standards and best practices Ensure that laboratory facilities and users are compliant with requisite operational policies and procedures (including, but not limited to health and safety regulations/guidelines for employees, students, and research participants; and ethics and human subjects research rules) Maintain operating knowledge and quality assurance/control in Kinesiology laboratory equipment, devices, and tools (i.e., Vicon Motion Capture, K4B2, Parvo Medics TruOne, Xsens, Force Plates, Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis, Gymaware, EMG, Ultrasound) utilized for teaching, learning, and research (including collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data) Responsible for installation, maintenance, repair, management, and troubleshooting of equipment and computing software and hardware for laboratory equipment Serve as resource for operation of laboratory facilities related to instructional/course needs Responsible for hiring, onboarding, and supervising students employed for duties/responsibilities associated with the department\u2019s laboratory facilities Organize and lead activities that facilitate collaborative research among and between Kinesiology students and faculty, internal and external stakeholders Program Operations (25%) Liaise with faculty and staff responsible for college- and university-level operations, especially the College\u2019s Deans\u2019 Office, Office of the Registrar and Office of Admissions, to facilitate success of academic programs and ensure department administrators, faculty, and staff are informed of current, updated academic programs related information, policies, and procedures Support the coordination of undergraduate internship processes and activities in collaboration with the Internship Coordinator, faculty, students, and external partners, including assisting with the development and maintenance of relationships with internship sites and community partners. Execute (routine and ad hoc) processes and procedures for course catalog building and modifications, in collaboration with department and college administrators/faculty Manages class schedule building processes, working closely with department and college administrators and staff (as relevant); including collaborating with faculty to arrange and revise course scheduling Assist department and college administrators with developing and implementing program review and improvement initiatives, including program assessment, evaluation, and strategic planning Assist in organizing and executing department faculty and staff retreat(s) and meetings; assist with college retreat Onboard and orient new and adjunct faculty in collaboration with college-level staff and administrators (as relevant) Manages faculty and staff professional development requests and reimbursements Assists in development of yearly departmental budgets and producing financial reports and summaries Student Recruitment and Success (20%) Coordinate and staff student recruitment and orientation events and activities (including occasionally outside of business hours) in coordination with college and university programming/initiatives Manage and maintain student records and database (including individual-level information related to personal contact, academic standing, and program progress) Monitor graduate and undergraduate student progress and retention, in partnership with academic advisors; diagnosing actual and potential student issues regarding fulfilling program requirements and compliance with academic policies and procedures Collaborate in efforts to assist students with compliance with relevant student handbooks and policies Support academic advisors and faculty advisors in guiding students with timely completion of program-related forms and currency of documentation of academic milestones and achievements As appropriate, keeps department administrator(s), faculty, department and college staff, and academic advisors appraised and updated on student progress at the individual- and cohort-level Facilitate, as needed and appropriate, communication between students and staff, faculty, or administrators at the department, college, and university levels Communications and Marketing (5%) Monitor and maintain department-related internet and intranet sites to assure accurate, up-to-date information Ensure department-related marketing and communication assets, especially information related to academic programs, are current and accessible across various forms of media and channels (digital, print, web, social media, etc.) Assist in obtaining information and content about pursuits and accomplishments of faculty, staff, students, alumni, and external partners across department programs, for college- and university-level marketing, communications, and advancement staff Maintain and provide (historic and current) information related to departmental academic programs, events, and systems QUALIFICATIONS: \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Bachelor\u2019s degree.Graduate degree (Master\u2019s or Doctoral) in discipline/field related to Kinesiology is preferred Demonstrated administrative and operational support experience.Experience working in an academic environment is preferred Strong collaborative skills, including ability to work in team environment Ability to communicate effectively through written, oral, and interpersonal ways Ability to organize, prioritize, and perform multiple tasks and responsibilities to facilitate completion Demonstrated commitment to diversity and inclusivity in line with the university\u2019s mission, vision and values All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $72,500 - $75,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Instructor--Biology_JR100318-1": {"title": "Instructor, Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nPosition Description The Department of Biology at Seattle University invites applications for a full-time Instructor position for the 2026-27 academic year. This position is a temporary replacement for current faculty on sabbatical leaves. We are seeking an instructor to teach some combination of introductory courses (BIOL 1610+1611, 1620+1621, 1630+1631), majors courses including genetics, anatomy and physiology, virology, and cell biology, and/or non-majors courses on cancer biology or global health. Specific course assignments will be determined based on the candidate\u2019s expertise and departmental needs. Ideal candidates will demonstrate expertise in a relevant field of biology and a commitment to undergraduate education and teaching excellence. Minimum requirements include a Masters degree, or equivalent, in a relevant biological field and undergraduate teaching experience. Salary Range: $55,000 - $61,000, dependent on experience Application Instructions: Submit applications to https://www.seattleu.edu/careers, and include a curriculum vitae; a cover letter that includes your future teaching plans and courses of interest; also include the names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin August 25, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Carolyn Stenbak (stenbakc@seattleu.edu). Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Salary Range: $55,000 - $61,000, dependent on experience. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100280": {"title": "Senior Administrative Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Essential Job Functions Kinesiology administrative support: Provide day-to-day administrative support for the Kinesiology program in coordination with the Program Chair and Program Manager. Responsibilities may include front desk support; maintaining lists of majors and minors, advising assignments, and email distribution lists; managing files, mail, and program records; coordinating faculty office needs and keys; monitoring and ordering office supplies; and providing other administrative support needed for the program. College financial and operational support: Work closely with the Director of Budget & Operations to support financial processing and reconciliation for the College of Nursing and Health Sciences. Review and track financial transactions, identify outstanding or incomplete items, and follow up with faculty, staff, and University offices as needed. Process and monitor reimbursements, invoice payments, purchasing transactions, and other financial activity in Workday through completion. Assist with routine financial reconciliation, documentation, and preparation of financial and operational reports. Support adjunct contracts, faculty payments, stipends, and related documentation as assigned. Academic scheduling and program support: Support the Kinesiology Program Chair and Program Manager with class scheduling and course information in University scheduling systems. Compile reports, schedule meetings, maintain program information, and provide administrative support for program projects and initiatives. Catalog and curriculum support: Assist the Kinesiology Program Chair and Program Manager with the annual catalog process and curriculum-related administrative work. Support preparation and routing of curriculum changes and maintain course and program information in University systems. Student support: Provide administrative support for Kinesiology students, including maintaining advising information, assisting with registration-related questions and processes, monitoring enrollment information, and maintaining student program records. Direct students to the appropriate faculty member, advisor, Program Chair, or Program Manager when needed. Faculty support: Provide routine administrative support to Kinesiology faculty, including room reservations, guest parking requests, catering, photocopying, scanning, and related needs. Assist with administrative aspects of faculty hiring and onboarding as assigned. Information management: Maintain program records, distribution lists, faculty and advising information, student resources, meeting minutes, and other program information. Assist with updates to program webpages, newsletters, and reports as needed. Events coordination and support: Provide administrative and logistical support for Kinesiology events, including space reservations, guest speaker travel and accommodations, catering, event promotion, payments, honoraria, and related documentation. Administrative support: Provide meeting coordination, preparation of materials, room reservations, technology coordination, meeting minutes, correspondence, and other administrative support to the Kinesiology Program Chair and Program Manager as needed. Other duties as assigned by the Kinesiology Program Chair, Program Manager, or Director of Budget & Operations. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent and two or more years of administrative office support experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the position. Proficiency in Microsoft Office programs, including Word, Excel, and Outlook, and willingness to learn University systems, including Workday. Experience maintaining a website or willingness to learn. Ability to communicate effectively in writing, by phone, and in person. Ability to work effectively with multiple stakeholders across the College and University. Excellent customer service skills and the ability to create a warm and welcoming environment. Strong collaborative and teamwork skills when working with students, faculty, staff, and University partners. Ability to organize, prioritize, and manage multiple responsibilities successfully. Strong attention to detail and ability to follow financial and administrative transactions through completion. Ability to maintain confidentiality, take direction, work independently, and problem-solve. All candidates must demonstrate a commitment to diversity and the University\u2019s mission, vision, and values. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree. Experience working in an academic environment or higher education. Experience with financial administration, reconciliation, reimbursements, invoice processing, or similar financial transactions. Experience with Workday or a similar financial/HR system. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance Salary Range: $22,737 \u2013 $23,800 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good-faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, dental and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short- and long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. Employees may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Seattle University also offers a generous holiday schedule, including a paid holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Lemieux-Library/Temporary-Copy-Cataloger--Part-Time--Lemieux-Library-and-McGoldrick-Learning-Commons_JR100306": {"title": "Temporary Copy Cataloger (Part-Time) Lemieux Library and McGoldrick Learning Commons", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nPosition Description Seattle University seeks a creative and collaborative Part-Time Temporary Copy Cataloger to focus on the physical copy cataloging of two major gift collections (2000+ gifts) for the Lemieux and Cornish Libraries. \u00a0Working primarily in Ex Libris Alma, this position will locate, evaluate, import, and adapt bibliographic records from the Orbis Cascade Alliance Network Zone (NZ) into our local Institution Zone (IZ) to ensure physical collections are accurately represented and discoverable via Primo VE. \u00a0\u00a0The successful candidate will possess strong technical cataloging skills, experience working in shared bibliographic environments, and the ability to work independently while maintaining a high level of accuracy. This faculty position, at the rank of Assistant Librarian, reports to the Director, Resource Acquisition and Management (RAM).\u00a0 Part-time (20 hours per week). This temporary project is expected to be completed by December 19, 2026. Key Responsibilities Copy Cataloging & Import into Ex Libris Alma (75%) Follow local gift procedures to search existing bibliographic records in the shared Alma Institutional Zone (IZ) and Network Zone (NZ) to identify any duplicate holdings in Cornish and Lemieux Libraries Appropriately link, match, and copy bibliographic records to Institutional Zone (IZ) from the Network Zone (NZ) following consortium and local rules to prevent record duplication. Import bibliographic records from OCLC (using Connexion or WorldShare) into the Network Zone (NZ) when appropriate and in accordance with consortium policies.\u00a0 Edit and upgrade MARC records using Alma\u2019s Metadata Editor and OCLC to comply with current cataloging standards including:\u00a0 Resource Description and Access (RDA), and Library of Congress Subject Headings (LCSH). Verify and assign appropriate Library of Congress (LC) classification as well as local call numbers Route materials requiring original cataloging, preservation review, or other specialized processing according to established local workflows. Physical Processing & Inventory Maintenance (15%) Create and update physical holdings records and item records in the Institution Zone (IZ) and inputting data including barcodes, copy numbers, shelving locations, and material types, etc., Complete end procession including spine labels, barcodes, and security strips as needed Project Tracking & Collaboration (10%) Track and report weekly processing statistics to the Director of RAM Coordinate with permanent staff to resolve conflicts between local IZ policies and shared NZ metadata. Physical & Work Environment Demands Work Location: On-site at the First Hill or South Lake Union campuses. Physical Tasks: Ability to sit or stand at a computer workstation for extended periods; capability to lift and move bins or carts of books weighing up to 25 lbs. Qualifications & Skills Required: Education: Bachelor\u2019s degree required; coursework or certificate in library science or database maintenance preferred. Experience: Minimum of 1\u20132 years of experience handling copy cataloging workflows in an academic library. Alma Expertise: Hands-on experience navigating Ex Libris Alma, specifically utilizing the Metadata Editor for single-record imports and managing records across the Institution Zone (IZ) and Network Zone (NZ). Technical Knowledge: Proficiency with MARC21 formatting, OCLC WorldCat database searching, and Library of Congress Classification (LCC) standards.\u00a0 Preferred: Master of Library Science (MLS/MLIS) from an ALA-accredited institution (or current enrollment in a graduate program). Previous experience working within an academic library consortium using a shared Alma Network Zone.\u00a0 Seattle University\u2019s Lemieux Library and McGoldrick Learning Commons is committed to recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty in affirmation of our institutional values and the library\u2019s Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion Action Plan . Application Instructions Applicants must apply at https://www.seattleu.edu/human-resources/careers/ and provide: (1) detailed letter of application identifying the candidate\u2019s education, experience, achievements, potential to succeed in the position\u037e (2) comprehensive curriculum vitae\u037e (3) statement on how the candidate, personally and professionally, can contribute to the mission, vision, and values of Seattle University\u037e (4) names and contact information for three professional references who can address the candidate\u2019s experience and ability pertinent to the position. (Letters may be solicited upon submission of application). Application review begins immediately. Open until filled. Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Adjunct--Biology_JR100308": {"title": "Adjunct, Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nPosition Description The Department of Biology at Seattle University invites applications for part - time Adjunct Faculty position s for the 2026-27 academic year , starting in September 2026, January 2027, and March 2027 . \u202fTh ese position s are temporary replacement s for current faculty on sabbatical leaves. We are seeking instructor s to teach introductory courses (BIOL 1610+1611, 1620+1621, 1630+1631), majors courses including genetics, anatomy and physiology, virology, and cell biology, and /or non- majors courses on cancer biology or global health. Specific course assignments will be determined based on the candidate\u2019s expertise and departmental needs. Ideal candidates will demonstrate expertise in a relevant field of biology and a commitment to undergraduate education and teaching excellence. \u00a0 Submit applications to\u202f https://www.seattleu.edu/careers , and include a curriculum vitae; a cover letter that includes your future teaching plans and courses of interest; also include the names and contact information for three references. \u202fReview of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position s are filled . Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Carolyn Stenbak (stenbakc@seattleu.edu).\u202f \u00a0 Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Adjunct--Physics_JR100281": {"title": "Adjunct, Physics", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nPart time\n\nPosition Description Position Description \u00a0 The Physics Department at Seattle University is inviting applications fo r two \u00a0 part-time adjunct \u00a0faculty \u00a0 for \u00a0 the 2026-27 academic year .\u00a0 \u00a0 Position 1 \u00a0 will \u00a0 teach one section of \u00a0 algebra -based introductory mechanics, PHYS 1 050 \u00a0 in Fall Quarter and two sections of introductory physics laboratory in each of Fall, Winter, and Spring Quarters. \u00a0 Position 2 \u00a0 will teach 4 sections of \u00a0 introductory \u00a0 physics laboratory in Fall Quarter only. \u00a0 The instructor s \u00a0 must be available on campus \u00a0 for in-person instruction and office hours. \u00a0 \u00a0 Minimum \u00a0 Qualifications \u00a0 Master's degree in physics or a related discipline; or bachelor's degree plus three years graduate study in physics \u00a0 Demonstrated interest or experience teaching at the college level \u00a0 \u00a0 Application Instructions \u00a0 Applicants should submit applications online at \u00a0 www.seattleu.edu/careers , including a \u00a0 cover letter and \u00a0 Curriculum Vitae/Resume . \u00a0 Open until filled. \u00a0 \u00a0 Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Facilities-Painter_JR100143": {"title": "Facilities Painter", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Operations and Maintenance (90%) Perform all phases of interior and exterior maintenance painting. Clean and prepare all surfaces before painting and perform any needed surface repairs. Preparations may include washing, sanding, pressure washing, masking, spackling, caulking, drywall taping, and other processes as needed. Apply appropriate primers, paints, sealers, stains, and other finishes as needed. Use brush, roll, and spray application methods as needed. Rig and operate scaffolds, lifts, and stages for work at heights up to 150 feet. Provide direction and training to temporary workers. Perform other duties of a similar nature or level. Plan and organize assigned work to fulfill task standards, meet schedule requirements, and maintain workload balance. Work autonomously or as part of a team, depending on the project or problem being addressed. Properly and safely use appropriate tools used in performing required tasks. Account for and maintain university-owned tools and equipment; repair and maintain tools as needed. Maintain a safe and clean shop space. Follow and promote established safety rules and regulations; attend safety trainings as required. Work in a safe, clean and respectful manner while performing work on campus. Work in occupied spaces with minimal disruption of campus activities. Scheduling (3%) Schedule work taking into account customer requests, space available and potential disruption to adjacent academic, work and living areas. Communication and Customer service (4%) Communicate with requester regarding assessment, planning, scheduling, and progress of work. Provide timely and ongoing notification to all parties affected by work. Coordinate with Facilities managers on project request recommendations, estimates, and work management reports as required. Administrative (3%) Open, update, track and complete all work orders assigned. Complete all paperwork and tracking required for personal time sheets in a timely manner. Attend departmental meetings as required. Marginal Functions Supervise student workers Minimum Qualifications High school diploma and four years experience as a painter or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $32.64 per hour - $37.64 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Associate-Director--Center-for-Professional-Development---Private-Practice_JR100207-1": {"title": "Associate Director, Center for Professional Development - Private Practice", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Supporting students and alumni in their professional development endeavors (60%) The Associate Director will focus on supporting students and alumni interested in establishing a career in private practice with law firms of all sizes. The Associate Director may also assist in supporting students and alumni pursuing positions in JD Advantage roles. The Associate Director will serve as the Department\u2019s main contact for students interested in pursuing opportunities at large corporate law firms (Big Law). The Associate Director will also serve as the main contact for students interested in learning more about in-house counsel career paths. The Associate Director will serves the Department\u2019s main contact for the law school\u2019s TILE (Technology, Innovation Law, and Ethics) Institute. The Associate Director will work with students interested in and qualified to pursue intellectual property career paths. The Associate Director counsels law students and alumni on career options, preparation of application materials, job search skills, and professional development/networking opportunities. As a CPD team member, the Associate Director will utilize their writing skills to review, edit, and identify areas of improvement on job application materials, including resumes, cover letters, and personal statements. Document review in CPD requires the ability to manage a high volume of professional correspondence and edit hundreds of resumes, cover letters, personal statements, and other materials. The Associate Director will meet one-on-one with students to develop personalized career support strategies. During busy times of the academic year, each CPD team member may meet with up to 6 to 8 students/graduates per day. The Associate Director will document their student/alumni meetings with substantive counseling notes in Symplicity, CPD\u2019s Career Management System. The Associate Director assists with the development of CPD\u2019s electronic communications with students and graduates; in particular, the Associate Director will create content for a bi-weekly CPD newsletter for a specific class level, targeted emails, and content updates for the CPD website. Other duties as requested. Creating substantive programming and collaborating with external organizations (25%) The Associate Director will serve as CPD\u2019s primary point of contact for private law firms in the city, region, state, and across the country. The Associate Director will also serve as a CPD point of contact for a number internal/external organizations and collaborative programs. These organizations and programs include, but are not limited to: Seattle Area Legal Recruiting Administrators (SALRA) The National Association for Law Placement (NALP) The Legal Mentor Network The Joint Minority Mentorship Program (JMMP) The Associate Director will be responsible, with the support of CPD\u2019s Program Coordinator, for managing and overseeing all of CPD\u2019s interview programs that are held throughout the calendar year (summer/fall/spring) and created for our recent graduate population. The Associate Director will organize and oversee CPD\u2019s annual Summer Associate networking event held every fall with local/regional law firms. The Associate Director will collaborate with CPD colleagues, SBA student organizations, School of Law departments, alumni, and community lawyers to develop and promote relevant and engaging workshops and presentations for the law students and the law school community (virtual & in person). The Associate Director will engage in public speaking on a routine basis. The Associate Director will analyze and develop student resources, including surveys, reports, and databases; the Associate Director will author and co-author a broad range of job search materials, including resources for resumes, cover letters, interviewing, and networking. The Associate Director will stay up-to-date and apprised of developments and trends in the legal profession and in the professional development world, particularly in the areas of application materials, interviewing, market trends, private practice hiring, and the specific substantive practice areas they advise on. The Associate Director will regularly represent CPD at community events with law firms, corporations, organizations, and regional bar associations. This may require attendance at evening events and other programs that fall outside of traditional business hours. Developing effective working relationships with these groups is crucial to the operations of CPD. Other duties as requested. Assisting with data management and graduate data collection (15%) The Associate Director will work closely with the Assistant Dean and CPD team to collect, analyze, and submit employment statistics for students and graduates for institutional, accreditation, and annual reporting requirements. The Associate Director will be assigned a specific number of recent graduates to track and support every year. The Associate Director will assist with collecting and managing student data contained in Symplicity, CPD\u2019s Career Management System. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree. Exceptional written and oral interpersonal communication skills. Applicants must be effective in one-on-one counseling situations and comfortable in larger groups as a leader in making public presentations. Program management experience. Self-motivation and individual initiative to manage duties/projects, as well as the desire and ability to work effectively as a member of a team. Ability to participate and attend local and regional events. Such events will include some evening and weekend commitments. The ability to remain knowledgeable of available job opportunities and hiring practices within private law firms and businesses. Applicants must be able to effectively communicate this knowledge to students and alumni as they apply for these highly competitive positions. Experience with Microsoft Office products and Sharepoint. Commitment to professional development and growth. Preferred Qualifications Juris Doctor degree from an American Bar Association accredited law school, plus two years of work experience practicing law in a private firm setting, including participation in the hiring of attorneys and/or law clerks \u2013 or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the Associate Director position. Experience with local, regional, and national hiring practices amongst private law firms and corporations. Career coaching and mentorship experience. Familiarity with ABA and NALP guidelines. Experience working with Symplicity or other similar Career Management System. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $78,000 - $85,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Cornish-Playhouse/Technical-Director-Shop-Supervisor_JR100215-1": {"title": "Technical Director/Shop Supervisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Essential\u202fFunctions Supervise Technical shop staff, including Costume Shop Supervisor, Paint and Props Supervisor, and ATD/Scene Shop Supervisor.\u00a0 Prepare and draft all technical drawings for\u00a0productions\u00a0and\u00a0construction.\u00a0 Prepare, coordinate, and\u00a0be responsible for\u00a0the\u00a0supervision of\u00a0all personnel,\u00a0students,\u00a0stagehands,\u00a0equipment,\u00a0and services related to physical production.\u00a0 Prepare and\u00a0coordinate\u00a0the load-in/load-out of all sets and props,\u00a0the\u00a0technical aspects of rehearsals,\u00a0and\u00a0the\u00a0run of shows\u00a0in performance venues.\u00a0 Develop, advise on, and track budgets for all technical department activities.\u00a0 Attend tech\u202fand dress\u202frehearsals\u202ffor University\u202fproductions.\u00a0 \u202fSet and\u00a0enforce\u00a0safety guidelines\u202fand familiarity with OSHA safety standards.\u00a0 \u202fIn conjunction\u00a0with Faculty, the\u00a0Production\u00a0Manager, and the\u00a0Shop\u00a0Supervisor,\u00a0create\u202fshop timelines inclusive of design deadlines plus associated deliverables.\u00a0 \u202fCollaborate\u202fwith\u202ffaculty,\u00a0guest\u202fdirectors,\u00a0and\u202fdesigners.\u00a0 \u00a0 Qualifications \u202fMinimum of\u00a05\u00a0years of related\u00a0progressive\u00a0experience.\u00a0 Must be familiar with all aspects of\u202ftechnical theatre\u202fproduction.\u00a0 Experience and/or working knowledge of theatrical\u00a0safety\u00a0protocols and systems.\u00a0 Expert\u202fknowledge of\u202ftheatrical scene shop\u202ftool use and maintenance.\u202f\u00a0 Expertise\u202fin welding, rigging, and\u202fcarpentry\u202ffor theatrical scenery.\u00a0 Basic\u202fknowledge of\u202ftheatrical\u202fshow automation,\u202flighting\u202f& sound\u202fsystems, electrical concepts,\u202fpaints, and props.\u00a0 Computer Skills:\u202fProficient in\u00a0AutoCAD,\u202fVectorWorks,\u00a0creating\u00a0CNC\u00a0drawings and programming, as well as the\u00a0Microsoft\u202fOffice\u202fSuite.\u00a0 Good working knowledge/basic skills\u202fin financial administration\u202f&\u202fmaintenance of\u202ftheatre production\u202fbudgets.\u00a0 Experience in supervising\u202fcrews,\u202ftraining\u202fand leading\u202fteams of\u202fworkers (work study students)\u202fwith varying levels of experience\u202fin\u00a0the safe operations\u202fof\u202fall\u00a0shop, rigging, lighting, and sound equipment.\u00a0 Excellent\u00a0verbal,\u00a0written, and visual\u00a0communication\u202fskills;\u202fanalytical ability\u202frequired.\u00a0 Participation in program and department planning meetings.\u00a0 Highly developed organizational skills and attention to detail.\u00a0 Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to lead teams effectively.\u00a0 Proven ability to collaborate with artistic staff and colleagues.\u00a0 Ability to work nights and weekends as needed for\u00a0productions.\u00a0 Self-motivated with excellent time management and the ability to prioritize.\u00a0 This position requires a criminal history background check.\u00a0 Valid Driver\u2019s license.\u00a0 Preferred Qualifications \u202fMFA or equivalent degree/professional experience\u202fin a theatrical or related field.\u00a0 \u202fWorking with students in a mentorship/instructional\u202fcapacity.\u00a0 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $28.72 per hour - $31.38 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Director--University-Events_JR100115": {"title": "Assistant Director, University Events", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Position Description Responsible for the project management of the university\u2019s signature events, this position professionally plans, produces, and analyzes key events hosted by Seattle University to further the university\u2019s educational mission, strategic, and business plans. The Assistant Director of University Events oversees all elements of in-person, hybrid, and virtual event management including but not limited to vision and goal setting, audience development, guest management, event marketing, programming and script development, speaker coaching, production specifications, coordination of catering and beverage service, venue and vendor management, and event staffing. Supporting University Events strategic planning, the Assistant Director develops and refreshes event management protocols and procedures, leads event debriefing, and produces comprehensive post-event analysis. Position Summary Reporting to the Sr. Director of University Events, the Assistant Director of University Events project manages fundraising, cultivation, engagement, and stewardship events to engage, deepen relationships, and recognize Seattle University constituents including but not limited to alumni, donors, faculty, staff, students, parents, and friends. This role is responsible for using expertise to partner with internal clients and subject matter experts to successfully implement impactful, engaging events that elevate the image of Seattle University. This role is responsible for the coordination of Seattle University\u2019s most critical mission-driven VIP engagements including but not limited to events sponsored by the Offices of Advancement, the President, the Provost, and the Board of Trustees. The Assistant Director will demonstrate exceptional leadership qualities, be mission-centered, have an open, creative problem-solving approach, strong relationship-building skills, and a strong understanding of and experience with building and supporting diverse and inclusive teams. Expert knowledge of event planning and coordinating, program development, and event production is necessary. The position requires a collaborative, resourceful and strategic thinker. Essential Job Functions The Assistant Director of University Events has the following areas of responsibility: Event Planning and Coordination\u00a0 Responsible for high level professional production of signature university events to cultivate new donors, steward existing donors, engage students, faculty, staff and alumni, and strategically raise and increase support for the university\u2019s mission and programs. Collaborate with university leaders, development officers, alumni engagement officers, and subject matter experts to develop event vision and goals, messaging, team roles and responsibilities, program outlines, and communication timelines. Leverage project management tools to develop accurate event/project timelines and budgets, coordinate with on- and off-campus partners to manage venue contacts, catering partners, entertainment, parking, A/V, and other event logistics. Lead event program development, draft speaker briefs, identify and interview potential program speakers, coach program speakers, write talking points and/or scripts, and act as stage manager as assigned. Working with the Sr. Director for University Events and University Marcom team to oversee the development of event messaging including, but not limited to: save the date, invitation, social media, and event communication content. In collaboration with MarCom, project manage video storytelling projects as needed for event production. Coordinate event staffing plans and communications/trainings for large\u00adscale events. Recruit and train event leads to implement staffing plans on event day. Lead events colleagues and advancement teams in identifying student ambassadors to serve as volunteers for relevant events. Lead event stakeholders in formal and informal event debriefs. Gather qualitative and quantitative event results, analyze performance, distribute event wrap reports, and share recommendations to improve future event performance. Event Standards and Systems Coordination\u00a0 Lead development and implementation of best practices for event coordination. Promote awareness and serve as a resource among the division and across the university for standard event protocols including maintaining records and guest management. Lead event briefing production as assigned including drafting event briefs, speaker briefs, and presidential briefs. Utilize web and database tools such as Microsoft Teams Planner, Social Tables, Eventbrite, and Raiser\u2019s Edge to effectively document and track event planning progress and data. Develop surveys for key event follow-up to create a feedback loop for event guests and help improve event experience. Prepare reports and analyze event data to document engagement value and ROI. Ensure that University Events coordination operations comply with the strategic goals and vision of the University. Along with the Sr. Director, create and implement short- and long-term strategic plans for University Events, including operating plans and continuous process improvement. Lead continual improvement efforts specific to event project management and data management systems. Remain current with event trends to inform event design and continually improve event program effectiveness. Contribute to annual University Events assessment to ensure departmental goals are met and adjust objectives as needed. Marginal Job Functions Program Management\u00a0 Support the Sr. Director of University Events in developing and defining the university\u2019s portfolio of events and strategies that will achieve year to year and campaign goals. Evaluates and builds university capacity for future events in alignment with the needs of the department and university to achieve agreed upon goals. Acts as a lead contact for the University Events team among campus departments and units. Partner and Vendor Relationship Management\u00a0 Develops and maintains excellent event stakeholder relationships, facilitating the success of programs held on the Seattle University campus. In coordination with the Sr. Director of University Events, adjusts University Events operations as needed to better serve customers and partners. Negotiates agreements and ensures partners/vendors have smooth and mutually beneficial communications with University Events. Regularly assess external vendors to ensure that Seattle University is continually given excellent rates and services. Negotiate agreements and prepare contracts for external vendor services, equipment, and support. Acts as single point of contact for vendors. Review vendor invoices for accuracy and provide financial data and reconciliation to involved departments. Attends assigned or appropriate ad hoc meetings with service providers and provides activity reports to University Events management as needed. Ensure that policies and procedures related to service provider relations are followed and recommend changes as necessary. \u200b Other duties as assigned \u00a0 and as \u00a0 relevant to the success of events and the University Events team. Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree plus three years\u2019 relevant experience, or 5+ years in event coordination, management, or hospitality. Exceptional project management with competing timelines, priorities, and limited budgets. Exemplary attention to detail. Successful candidates will have strong customer service skills and mindset. Familiarity with technologies, platforms, systems, and analytics. Skill in big-picture thinking and strategic planning as well as presenting new ideas, strategies, and initiatives both proactively and responsively. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, the ability to give presentations, lead meetings, and contribute to groups and gatherings of varied sizes. Ability to operate creatively, strategically, and consciously in a dynamic, intercultural organization with many communication considerations rooted in culture and tradition. Understanding of current event trends and best practices. All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to diversity and the university's mission, vision, and values. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in hospitality, Communications, Marketing, Business, or related field. Individual is inspired to work in alignment with the university\u2019s mission and values. Experience working among diverse communities and equity and inclusion initiative work. Work experience in higher education and/or nonprofit or public sector organizations. Experience working in collegiate event environment. Proven track record of high-level communications with a diverse range of clients, attention to detail and policy enforcement. Proficiency in Social Tables, Event Management System (EMS), Localist, Raiser\u2019s Edge, and other event software or platforms. A successful candidate will have work experience that demonstrates their ability to work independently and collaboratively given guidelines and priorities. University Advancement is committed to developing an equitable and inclusive culture that fosters a sense of belonging for all. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume\u00a0when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified.\u00a0 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $63,000 - $67,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Associate-Director-of-Development--Annual-Giving_JR100197": {"title": "Associate Director of Development, Annual Giving", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: The \u00a0Associate Director will work closely with the AVP Development, \u00a0AVP of Advancement Services, and the Director of Prospect Development to develop a strategy and portfolio of discovery prospects. RESPONSIBILITIES Donor Relations & Communication (80%) The Associate Director identifies, cultivates, and solicits leadership annual gifts while actively stewarding prospects through proposal development stages in conformity with the university\u2019s prospect management protocol. Establish fundraising goals and provide reports on progress to partners within University Advancement. Annually solicit a portfolio of 150+ assigned prospects, securing annual gifts in the $1,000-$25,000 range, using multi-channel communication tools, including email, social media, ThankView (video), and texting; in addition to in-person or virtual one-on-one meetings. Promote philanthropic opportunities, such as university and college giving priorities, crowdfunding projects, Seattle U Gives, President\u2019s Club, and other annual giving campaigns Build, manage, and engage a donor portfolio for current and future solicitations. Prioritize personal visits with identified prospects. Establishes fundraising goals and reports on progress. Make qualification face-to-face visits beyond those in the portfolios (discovery). As needed, arrange and lead visits for donors and prospects with key university partners, including alumni, and industry stakeholders. Works as part of the Annual Giving team and with UA annual giving staff regarding annual giving efforts such as SU Gives and direct mail. Responsible for securing matchings and other incentives to amplify giving. Ensures annual donor impact reporting is correct and timely for endowed and recurring gifts. As needed, ensures timely and appropriate acknowledgement for all gifts, verifies that gifts are properly recorded in the appropriate fund and are documented in the university's gift processing database Strategic Alignment and Institutional Knowledge (15%) Work with Advancement and Seattle University staff to plan strategies for cultivation and solicitation of prospective donors for annual gifts as well as transitioning donors who are ready to move into major gift pools/portfolios. Understand and track the progress towards achieving SU\u2019s Strategic Directions. Develop and maintain broad knowledge of the university, such as Jesuit/Catholic traditions and values, admissions, athletics, colleges, and continuing education. Learn the values and programs that make Seattle University different from other area universities. Develop a network of contacts throughout the university to access for additional information. Build out resource hub of relevant content to share with donors Other Duties (5%) Attend trainings and meetings for University Advancement. Undertake additional special projects as directed. Perform other duties as assigned. MINIMUM Qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s degree and 3+ years\u2019 work experience in fundraising/development, higher education, alumni or customer engagement, non-profit program management, marketing/sales, or similar fields. High level of cultural competency including an understanding and sensitivity to issues of race, class, and the ethical dilemmas that may arise from the development of strong reciprocal partnerships with alumni, donors, friends of the university, students, and faculty and staff. Ability to communicate effectively, orally and in writing, with diverse audiences including university leadership, donors, students, faculty and staff, and a diverse alumni population. Strong computer skills, particularly working with Microsoft Word, Excel, and Outlook. Attention to accuracy and detail while accomplishing multiple tasks. Ability to maintain confidentiality, communicate with sensitivity, and follow university protocols. Ability to manage a complex set of assignments through strong analytical, planning, reporting, project management, and collaboration skills. Preferred Qualifications : We are looking for candidates who believe the power of diversity enriches all of us by exposing us to a range of ways to understand and engage with the world, identify challenges, and to discover, design, and deliver solutions. Familiarity with customer/donor relationship management software such as Raiser\u2019s Edge, or willingness and ability to learn new software. University Advancement is committed to developing an equitable and inclusive culture that fosters a sense of belonging for all. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $72,000 - $82,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Associate-Director--Development-Alumni-Relations--School-of-Law_JR100198": {"title": "Associate Director, Development/Alumni Relations, School of Law", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: PURPOSE The Associate Director of Development is responsible for identifying, qualifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding alumni, donors, and prospective donors in support of the Seattle University School of Law. The Associate Director manages a portfolio spanning leadership annual giving and major gift prospects, with an emphasis on building the donor pipeline, increasing philanthropic support, and strategically transitioning donors toward greater levels of engagement and investment. The successful candidate will demonstrate strategic alignment, collaboration, and institutional knowledge by building strong relationships across the School of Law and Seattle University and connecting donor interests with institutional priorities and the University\u2019s Jesuit Catholic mission. DEVELOPMENT (90%): Build, manage, and actively engage a portfolio of assigned prospects and donors, developing individualized strategies that move donors from qualification and cultivation through solicitation and stewardship. Annually solicit a portfolio of 150+ assigned prospects, securing leadership annual gifts generally in the $1,000\u2013$25,000 range while identifying and advancing donors with capacity and inclination for major gifts. Identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward major gift prospects in support of School of Law and University priorities, in close collaboration with the Assistant Dean, Dean, and University Advancement colleagues. Maintain an active pipeline of prospective major gift donors, including a Top 25 pool of prospects positioned for solicitation within the next 12\u201318 months. Complete qualification and discovery visits beyond the assigned portfolio to identify new philanthropic prospects and expand the School of Law donor pipeline. Develop and submit 12\u201325 major gift proposals annually and steward prospects through the proposal and solicitation process in accordance with University prospect management protocols. Use a strategic mix of in-person and virtual meetings and multi-channel communications\u2014including email, video, social media, texting, and other appropriate tools\u2014to deepen donor engagement and increase philanthropic support. Establish annual fundraising, activity, and pipeline goals in consultation with University Advancement leadership; develop annual work plans and regularly assess and report progress toward established goals. Promote philanthropic opportunities across the School of Law and University, including academic and programmatic priorities, endowed funds, scholarships, Seattle U Gives, leadership annual giving, matching opportunities, and other strategic fundraising initiatives. Partner with the Dean, Assistant Dean, faculty, academic leadership, alumni, volunteers, and other key stakeholders to cultivate and solicit individual and organizational donors, including law firms, bar associations, corporations, and other partners. Prepare and position the Dean, President, faculty, and other University leaders for effective donor engagement through prospect strategy, briefing materials, talking points, proposals, stewardship reports, and appropriate follow-up. Arrange and lead donor and prospect meetings and visits with university leaders, faculty, alumni, industry partners, and other stakeholders as appropriate. Collaborate with the University Advancement Annual Giving team on School of Law annual giving strategies, including Seattle U Gives, direct response efforts, donor matching opportunities, volunteer engagement, and campaign messaging. Partner with Advancement Events & Stewardship and School of Law colleagues to develop meaningful cultivation and stewardship opportunities, including events, donor recognition, impact reporting, gift acknowledgements, and other personalized engagement. Support the planning and execution of donor and alumni events, including development of invitation strategies and guest lists, program content, leadership briefings, pre- and post-event engagement strategies, and measures of fundraising and engagement effectiveness. Ensure donors receive a positive and meaningful giving experience through timely acknowledgement, appropriate recognition, personalized stewardship, and communication about the impact of their philanthropy. In collaboration with UA Donor Relations, coordinate annual donor impact reporting for endowed, recurring, and other designated gifts and help ensure gifts are appropriately documented, acknowledged, and directed to the intended funds. Recommend strategies for donor- and philanthropy-related content across School of Law communications, including digital communications, publications, donor stories, and other engagement channels. Manage prospects in accordance with university prospect management and clearance protocols, coordinating prospective donor activity across University Advancement to maximize philanthropic opportunity and provide a consistent donor experience. Maintain timely and accurate records of donor and prospect interactions, strategies, proposals, and activity in the University's advancement database, including substantive engagement by the Dean, University leadership, faculty, and volunteers. Other Duties (10%) Attend trainings and meetings for University Advancement. Undertake additional special projects as directed. Perform other duties as assigned. MINIMUM Qualifications: Bachelor\u2019s degree and 3+ years\u2019 work experience in fundraising/development, higher education, alumni or customer engagement, non-profit program management, marketing/sales, or similar fields. High level of cultural competency including an understanding and sensitivity to issues of race, class, and the ethical dilemmas that may arise from the development of strong reciprocal partnerships with alumni, donors, friends of the university, students, and faculty and staff. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal skills, along with the ability to work professionally with members of the external community and be comfortable in situations that require public presentations in front of large groups. Ability to communicate effectively, orally and in writing, with diverse audiences including university leadership, donors, students, faculty and staff, and a diverse alumni population. Strong computer skills, particularly working with Microsoft Word, Excel, and Outlook. Attention to accuracy and detail while accomplishing multiple tasks. Ability to maintain confidentiality, communicate with sensitivity, and follow university protocols. Ability to manage a complex set of assignments through strong analytical, planning, reporting, project management, and collaboration skills. Preferred Qualifications : We are looking for candidates who believe the power of diversity enriches all of us by exposing us to a range of ways to understand and engage with the world, identify challenges, and to discover, design, and deliver solutions. Familiarity with customer/donor relationship management software such as Raiser\u2019s Edge, or willingness and ability to learn new software. Familiarity with the legal profession and community. Familiarity with planned giving. University Advancement is committed to developing an equitable and inclusive culture that fosters a sense of belonging for all . Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $72,000 - $82,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Adjunct--College-of-Nursing-and-Health-Sciences_JR100195": {"title": "Adjunct, College of Nursing and Health Sciences", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-11\nPart time\n\nPosition Description Position Description Seattle University College of Nursing and Health Sciences and Health Sciences invites applications for part-time adjunct faculty to teach at the BSN through DNP levels. \u00a0Applications are accepted throughout the year, with openings available in a variety of specialties.\u00a0 We seek outstanding nurses with at least one year of experience in their specialties to teach clinically in the following areas: \u00a0medical-surgical nursing, reproductive health nursing, pediatric nursing, psychiatric/mental health nursing, population health, and geriatric nursing. In addition, we welcome applicants with advanced practice certification and two years of clinical experience as a nurse practitioner to teach graduate students in systems leadership, midwifery, and nurse practitioner tracks including: Family, Adult-Gerontology (Acute and Primary Care), Women\u2019s Health, and Family Psychiatric Mental Health. \u00a0 Candidates must demonstrate commitment to equity and inclusion and the University\u2019s mission, vision and values. The College emphasizes care of underserved and marginalized populations, both locally and abroad. In concert with our Jesuit mission, we are dedicated to social justice and formation for leadership. The faculty is committed to graduating the highest quality nurses, both at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Nationally respected neighboring health and research institutions offer opportunities for collaboration; Seattle University faculty, staff and students are eager to engage in interdisciplinary work. \u00a0Additional information is available at www.seattleu.edu/nursing. \u00a0 Depending upon specialty and site, prelicensure clinical courses are taught from one to two days a week; eight hour or twelve-hour shifts; days, evenings, or nights; weekdays or weekends; and in a variety of clinical sites throughout King, Snohomish, and Pierce counties. \u00a0\u00a0 Qualifications Required: \u00a0A master\u2019s degree plus two years of clinical experience, with at least one year in the specialty is preferred for all faculty positions; however, applicants with a baccalaureate degree in nursing and a minimum of three years of experience in the specialty will be considered for prelicensure courses only. \u00a0Applicants should hold an unencumbered license to practice Washington State as a Registered Nurse. \u00a0Advanced practice faculty must also hold WA State licensure as an Advanced Registered Nurse Practitioner (ARNP) and have two years of experience in practice as an ARNP. Application Instructions Applicants should submit materials online at https://www.seattleu.edu/careers . Please include a cover letter of interest and qualifications, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, and contact information for three references. Application review will be ongoing. Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100096": {"title": "Senior Administrative Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Administrative Support\u202f\u00a0 Provide general administrative support to the department chairs and faculty as needed, including activities such as report and spreadsheet generation, data entry, copying, and scheduling and room reservations for department gatherings.\u00a0 Partner with the department chairs, faculty, staff, and students to help implement strategic department goals including recruitment and outreach events.\u00a0 Support and assist faculty with teaching activities such as procurement of necessary supplies, interacting with Information Technology for classroom needs, and collection of course materials (e.g. syllabi, course grades) for archival purposes.\u00a0 Facilitate and assist with the course evaluations for faculty as needed.\u00a0 Maintain and coordinate content on department website and social media in partnership with chairs, faculty and staff.\u00a0 Partner with the chairs and faculty to coordinate and assist in the planning of departmental events.\u00a0 Maintain necessary departmental and student files and records.\u00a0 Assist with the hiring and on-boarding of new faculty and staff members.\u00a0 Request Letters of Appointment for part-time faculty and assist with the hiring of student workers.\u00a0 Assist chairs and faculty with academic research using university systems to gather and track student data and statistics.\u00a0 Provide administrative support to the Electrical and Computer Engineering Advisory Board: take meeting minutes, plan meetings and events, communicate with members, coordinate scholarship, track donations.\u00a0 Recruit and facilitate activities and meetings for the Electrical and Computer Engineering Student Ambassadors with the chair. Budget Activity Oversight Provide accounting support, including:\u00a0 Track and balance financial records for department(s) budgets, including purchases, reimbursements, and Procard activity.\u00a0 Order and record all equipment purchases.\u00a0 Maintain financial records for department(s) including purchase requisitions and financial reports.\u00a0 Issue regular year-to-date spending reports for the department chair(s) and reconcile departmental finances monthly.\u00a0 Academic Support\u00a0 Assist with registration process and course enrollment as needed.\u00a0 Support annual class schedule, advising assignment and catalog building processes and management.\u00a0 Work closely with department chair to prepare and track paperwork associated with scheduling and curriculum changes.\u00a0 Enter course description edits, curriculum\u202frevisions, additions and deletions in the university\u2019s online academic catalog management system and submit to Academic Program Manager for approval.\u00a0 Work closely with department chair(s) to revise and enter class schedule and\u202fcourse specifics in Schedule Planner, the university\u2019s online scheduling program.\u00a0 Proctor exams as needed.\u00a0 Reception\u00a0 Provide reception services for the department. Interact with students, parents, faculty and visitors in person and on the phone.\u00a0 Respond to questions or refer students and others to appropriate contacts.\u00a0 Assist part time faculty and affiliates with using university software and logistics such as attaining keys, campus cards, parking passes, etc.\u00a0 Arrange facilities repairs when necessary.\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications High School diploma or equivalent (GED) and two or more years administrative office support experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above.\u202f\u00a0 Experience with MS Word, MS Excel, MS Windows, MS Outlook and Internet Explorer.\u202f\u00a0 Experience maintaining a website.\u00a0 Ability to communicate effectively with others in writing, by phone and in person.\u00a0 Excellent customer service skills including the ability to create a warm and welcoming environment and work with diverse populations.\u00a0 Ability to organize, prioritize and multi-task independently with success.\u202f\u00a0 All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to diversity and the university\u2019s mission, vision, and values.\u202f\u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree Experience working in an academic environment.\u00a0 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $45,000 - $55,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Network-Engineer-2_JR100007": {"title": "Network Engineer 2", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-30\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Essential Responsibilities Own the performance, reliability, and continuous improvement of enterprise Wi-Fi services across two campuses, supporting 1,700+ access points, guest networks, Mist Edge controllers, and diverse client devices. Administer and enhance wired network infrastructure, including Juniper and Cisco switching environments. Diagnose and resolve complex wired and wireless connectivity issues across a wide range of devices. Communicate clearly with end users and stakeholders throughout the troubleshooting lifecycle, setting expectations and providing timely updates. Manage critical network services including RADIUS authentication, DNS, DHCP, and IP address management (IPAM). Maintain and optimize network monitoring, alerting, and configuration management. Perform certificate management and updates across multi-vendor network platforms. Plan and execute network upgrades, patching, and lifecycle maintenance to ensure security and high availability. Participate in scheduled maintenance windows and respond to critical after-hours incidents as needed. Partner with internal teams to identify root causes, improve network performance, and enhance user experience.\u00a0 Contribute to documentation, process improvements, and operational best practices. Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in IT or related field, or equivalent experience. 4+ years supporting enterprise Wi-Fi environments with 200+ access points. 2+ years supporting LAN environments with 100+ managed switches including troubleshooting, software upgrades, and hardware support. Demonstrated Experience With Juniper Mist wireless and switching Cisco IOS switching VLANs, STP, PoE, and link aggregation IPv4, DNS, DHCP, SNMP OSPF Experience Troubleshooting Connectivity Across Desktop \u2013 Windows and Mac Mobile \u2013 Apple IOS, Android, Other Various IoT platforms Proactively manage user expectations by providing timely ticket updates and end-to-end communication throughout the troubleshooting lifecycle. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Tools Visio: logical and physical network diagrams Excel: formulas, data manipulation, text manipulation, VLOOKUP, conditional formatting Word: styling, tables, graphics, TOC, watermarking Outlook: calendar invites, tasks, contacts, autoreplies Demonstrated Ability to Manage multiple tasks simultaneously Shift your focus from one task to another based on support needs Meet deadlines with attention to detail and accountability Work independently and collaboratively Communicate technical information clearly and tactfully Exercise sound judgment in complex situations Must be able to lift 50 pounds and transport up to 100 pounds on a cart among campus buildings. Must be able to work on ladders (6-10 ft) to access network equipment. Hands-on experience with Juniper EX (4000/4400/4650) switches and Virtual Chassis JunOS and MX204 routers Cisco Catalyst switches 3850, 4510, 4500-X Demonstrated understanding of IPv4 subnetting (/20\u2013/24). IPv6 knowledge a plus. \u00a0SolarWinds Network Performance Monitor (NPM) and Network Configuration Manager (NCM) EfficientIP SolidServer (DDI) Jira or similar ticketing systems Familiarity with AWS Route 53 Relevant certifications: Juniper JNCIS-MistAI-Wireless, JNCIS-MistAI-Wired, JNCIS-ENT, Cisco CCNA Applicable training courses for the technologies listed in this job posting. Familiarity with Palo Alto firewalls and Panorama is a plus. Project management experience is a plus. Preferred Qualifications Experience designing and supporting networks in higher education environments. Advanced experience with Juniper Mist at scale (~8,000+ daily connections). Experience maintaining a guest Wi-Fi portal in Mist Location Requirement Candidates must live within a reasonable commute of our Seattle offices. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $92,000 - $122,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Campus-Public-Safety-Officer_JR100017": {"title": "Campus Public Safety Officer", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Months Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: The Campus Public Safety Officer is a vital member of our campus Public Safety team and serves as the first responder for the university under the direction of the Public Safety Supervisor or their designee. This dynamic, active role requires vigilance, quick decision-making, and engagement with the campus community. The Public Safety Officer is a uniformed, non-commissioned officer position that actively patrols campus grounds, provides essential medical and security aid, coordinates emergency services, documents critical incidents, and ensures the safety and well-being of students, faculty, staff, and visitors. This role is deeply focused on community service and mental health support, as well as providing assistance to the greater campus community. The Campus Public Safety Officer maintains a shift schedule that includes coverage during campus closures and holidays. Officers work collaboratively in teams of 5-6 personnel, including a supervisor and dispatcher. Our department is staffed 24 hours a day, seven days a week. Officers are assigned to one of our teams. Teams rotate between day and night shifts every six months to ensure balanced coverage. Campus Safety & Security Operations \u00a0(60%) Conduct proactive patrols to monitor security, prevent incidents, and ensure campus safety. Respond to non-emergency requests, provide campus escorts, and assist with general security concerns. Operate the communications center, including monitoring safety systems, CCTV, dispatching responders, and keeping records. Uphold Seattle University policies while maintaining a customer-service-focused approach. Emergency Response & Incident Management\u00a0 (20%) Serve as the first responder to emergencies, including medical incidents, accidents, and security threats. Provide immediate medical assistance and coordinate emergency services (EMS, fire, law enforcement). Document and maintain accurate records of incidents, ensuring compliance with legal and institutional policies. Communications Center Operations (10%) Operate the campus Public Safety communications center Monitor security and fire life safety systems and dispatch responders as needed Answer and triage campus emergency line calls, remaining calm and coordinating appropriate response Maintain computer-aided dispatch program to track calls, incidents, and officer activity Utilize campus video surveillance system to deter and respond to incidents Community Engagement & Collaboration\u00a0 (10%) Offer support and guidance to students and staff, focusing on mental health and well-being. Collaborate with other Seattle University departments to enhance campus safety and services. Other duties as assigned.\u00a0 Other Job Activity:\u00a0 Move. Communicate. Observe and recognize. Run. Outdoor Environment in all weather. Qualifications High school diploma and one or more years of previous security experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above. \u00a0Ability to perform tasks requiring bending, stooping, standing, kneeling, walking and running significant distances; restrain and take into custody another person; receive university sponsored self defense training; lift or otherwise move up to the weight of a person; climb stairs; occasionally work at heights above 30 feet. Must be able to perform duties in confined spaces and varying outdoor weather conditions. Ability to communicate effectively, in both oral and written form, including use of two-way radio, telephone, and in face-to-face contacts. Must be able to give or take direction in stressful or emergency situations. Must be at least twenty-one years of age in order to legally enter a licensed or unlicensed drinking establishment to enforce rules and regulations. Valid Washington State driver\u2019s license required. Certification in CPR and First Aid required within three months of hire. Must be 21 years of age or older. All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to diversity and the university\u2019s mission, vision, and values. All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in a related field. Higher education experience. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $25.94 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director-of-University-Recreation_JR100047": {"title": "Director of University Recreation", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: The Director of University Recreation is a highly visible campus leader responsible for advancing student wellbeing, belonging, engagement, and holistic development through a comprehensive portfolio of recreation, fitness, outdoor, aquatics, and competitive sports programs. This position provides strategic vision and leadership for one of the university's most dynamic student-facing operations and serves as a key partner in advancing Seattle University's mission to educate the whole person. The Director leads a multifaceted recreation enterprise that includes two recreation facilities totaling more than 180,000 square feet, an aquatics program with two pools, a comprehensive fitness program, competitive sports including intramural sports and more than 15 sport clubs, outdoor recreation programs, and a workforce of more than 100 student employees. The Director oversees a team of professional and graduate staff, manages significant operating and capital resources, and serves as a collaborative leader across the university. Leadership & Administration: 45% Lead, inspire, develop, and hold accountable a high-performing team of professional staff, graduate assistants, and student employees while fostering a culture of excellence, innovation, belonging, and continuous improvement. Plan, administer, manage, and reconcile departmental budgets. Develop and implement strategies to increase revenue generation across programs and facilities, including aquatics, memberships, fitness services, rentals, certifications, and fee\u2011based instructional programs. Identify and expand new revenue streams to support long\u2011term program sustainability and facility enhancement. Lead the evaluation, implementation, and ongoing management of recreation technology systems, including access control, scheduling platforms, member engagement tools, and departmental reporting infrastructure. Develop and present evidence-based budget requests to institutional leadership using program utilization data, peer benchmarking, and long-range financial modeling; advocate for funding levels that reflect the full scope of UREC's mission and community demand. Develop and execute short and long-term financial strategies including revenue forecasting, and planning for operating and capital budgets. Ensure compliance with all university budget and purchasing policies. Develop and operationalize a staffing plan that meets the needs of the department while adhering to the annual budget allocation. Develop strategic plans, goals, and measurable objectives for University Recreation. Respond to and resolve escalated member complaints and maintain accurate complaint records. Administer, develop, implement department policies, procedures, and risk management practice Strategic Leadership: 25% Provide visionary leadership for the future of university recreation at Seattle University, identifying opportunities for innovation, growth, enhanced student engagement, and operational excellence. Serve as a key leader within Student & Campus Life, contributing to divisional priorities, student success initiatives, and university-wide strategic planning efforts. Develop and operationalize a strategic plan to revise and elevate UREC operations and programs based on national best practices in the Seattle University context. Partner with UREC staff, Student & Campus Life team, and campus colleagues to ensure collaborative programming and operations. Solicit and respond to survey feedback from UREC constituents to enhance the user experience. Promote UREC programs and services to students, faculty, staff, and alumni. Advance equity, inclusion, and belonging within UREC by developing programs, policies, and practices that affirm the identities and needs of all community members, with particular attention to historically underrepresented and marginalized populations. Actively collaborate with faculty across academic programs to develop curricular and co\u2011curricular partnerships, including course\u2011embedded recreation experiences, wellness\u2011integrated learning, research collaborations, and practicum or internship opportunities. Engage in intentional partnerships with academic deans, departments, and faculty committees to align UREC initiatives with institutional learning outcomes and wellbeing goals. Serve as a visible campus leader who, along with the UREC staff, regularly engages with campus events, student programs, faculty gatherings, and university initiatives beyond UREC facilities. Collaborate with a variety of campus and academic partners, including Athletics, the Center for Student Involvement, Housing & Residence Life, the MOSAIC Center, Orientation Programs, Wellness & Health Promotion, and others to ensure the delivery of programs and services that meet the holistic needs of a diverse community. Participate in Student & Campus Life team and campus committees as directed by supervisor. Represent the team and the university to external constituents, including regional and national-level professional associations. Foster a culture of professional growth and continuous learning by supporting staff participation in NIRSA, NASPA, and other professional associations; facilitating ongoing training in risk management, inclusion, and emerging best practices; and modeling reflective and values-driven leadership. Facilities Management: 15% Provide strategic oversight of one of the university's largest student-facing physical assets, ensuring facilities are safe, welcoming, financially sustainable, and aligned with evolving student and community needs. Develop, negotiate, and maintain formal shared-use agreements and operational protocols with Athletics, academic departments, and external user groups governing scheduling, supervision responsibilities, liability, and risk management in shared facilities. Oversee a comprehensive departmental risk management program including emergency action plans, incident documentation, participant waivers, staff certification requirements, and facility safety compliance across all UREC programs and spaces, with particular attention to aquatic operations and shared-use environments. Supervise the operations, equipment, and maintenance needs of the 180,000 square foot Redhawk Center & Eisiminger Fitness Center, including 2 pools, a full-service fitness center, courts, functional training spaces, multi-purpose gym, locker rooms, and other spaces. Develop and facilitate long-range planning for facility enhancements and preventative maintenance plans via asset inventory practices. Work closely with Facilities and vendors to respond to annual and incidental equipment and facility maintenance needs. Assessment & Planning: 10% Oversee the department\u2019s efforts to assess the quality and effectiveness of programs and services. Develop and oversee implementation of new programs and services as indicated by student/member feedback. Stay attuned to best practices and emerging research and continually evaluate the department to ensure it meets or exceeds professional standards. Design and implement a comprehensive departmental assessment plan that includes participant satisfaction, program learning outcomes, facility utilization data, and benchmarking against peer institutions and CAS standards. Other Duties Assigned: 5% Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in recreation administrator or other comparable program and a minimum of three years previous experience administering an educational program, or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above. Evidence of ability to provide leadership and problem-solving skills in analyzing recreation needs of emerging adults, especially college students. Demonstrated experience supervising and developing professional staff and graduate students. Strong oral and written communication skills, including presentations and group facilitation. Availability to work occasional evenings and weekends. All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to diversity and the university\u2019s mission, vision and values. All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check. First Aid/CPR/AED Certification required within 90 days of hire and maintained throughout employment. Preferred Qualifications Master\u2019s degree in recreation administration, higher education/student affairs, or a closely related discipline. Five years of full-time professional experience, preferably in a higher education setting, with previous experience in administering a campus recreation program, or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance $88,000 - $92,750 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Temporary-Employment-Pool_JR100018": {"title": "Temporary Employment Pool", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-02\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: Work Model: Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Join Our Temporary Talent Pool Interested in temporary employment at the university? We'd love to hear from you! From time to time, we have temporary opportunities across a variety of departments and functional areas. These positions may vary in length, schedule, and responsibilities, depending on the needs of the university. By applying to this posting, you'll be considered for current and future temporary opportunities that match your skills and experience. If a position becomes available that aligns with your background, a member of the hiring team may contact you to discuss the opportunity. Please note that submitting an application to this talent pool does not guarantee employment or an interview. We encourage you to keep your application up to date and to apply directly for any specific positions that interest you. Thank you for your interest in joining our university community. We appreciate your desire to contribute to our mission and look forward to learning more about you. Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Compensation at a Glance Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Clinical-Professor-1_JR100009": {"title": "Assistant Clinical Professor-1", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-26\nFull time\n\nPosition Description The Department of Counseling in the College of Education invites applications for one full-time Assistant Clinical Professor position in the CACREP-accredited On-ground Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC) Program at Seattle University. We seek a justice-centered counselor educator who is committed to advancing issues of diversity, anti-racism, equity, and inclusion within the mental health counseling profession beginning September 1, 2026. The Counseling Department in the College of Education has four program tracks: (1) On-ground Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC), (2) Online CMHC, (3) On-ground School Counseling, and (4) Online School Counseling. Each program focuses on preparing emerging counselors to be social change agents and advocates for diversity, anti-racism, equity, and inclusion (DEI). \u00a0 This position requires faculty to teach on-campus at Seattle University.\u00a0 \u200b Successful candidates will join a social justice-oriented counseling program informed by Seattle University\u2019s commitments to diversity, equity, and inclusion (see \u00a0 LIFT SU: Inclusive Excellence Action Plan for Racial Equity and Antiracism \u00a0 - \u00a0 https://www.seattleu.edu/diversity/lift-su/ ). The counseling programs in the College of Education are leaders in preparing future change agents in counseling and is CACREP-accredited through 2026. This position requires faculty to teach on-campus at Seattle University.\u00a0 Responsibilities : An assistant clinical professor is responsible for providing justice-centered teaching and service to the program, the College, the university, and to the counseling profession at the national level. Opportunities for scholarship that advance issues of equity in community mental health. Faculty in the on-ground program teach graduate level counseling courses on the Seattle University campus across the eight CACREP core areas in clinical mental health and school counseling and provide support to the program through advising. Candidates must demonstrate commitment to the University's and College of Education\u2019s Jesuit mission, vision, and values. The College emphasizes engagement in teaching, research, and service that contributes to building and sustaining equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. In concert with our Jesuit mission and commitment to diversity and inclusion, candidates must demonstrate an understanding of components of a Jesuit education and its alignment with the counseling program\u2019s social justice mission. Minimum Requirements: Earned doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited program by August 15, 2026 Licensed Mental Health Counselor experience Full professional licensed mental health counseling in state of residence at time of application or apply for full licensure/certification within one year of hire Familiarity with CACREP accreditation standards and processes Experience and competence in teaching and clinical supervision of counseling graduate students Demonstrated commitment to social justice and anti-racism as evidenced by curriculum development, scholarly agenda, and/or community engagement and collaboration Commitment to collaborative professional relationships with faculty colleagues Demonstrated ability to integrate diversity, equity, and inclusion and theory-to-practice research in teaching, scholarship, and service Preferred Qualifications:\u00a0 Demonstrated ability to support diverse students Full professional licensed mental health counseling in state of residence at time of application or apply for full licensure within one year of hire Ability to teach both school counseling and clinical mental health counseling courses Professional experience as a licensed mental health counselor Experience and competence in teaching and clinical supervision of clinical mental health counseling graduate students. Sustained engagement with minoritized communities that are underrepresented in counseling and mental health care, and demonstrated ability to integrate this experience into program development and teaching Candidates with historically marginalized identities are strongly encouraged to apply Understanding and treatment of substance use and process addictions Application Instructions Applicants should submit applications online at www.seattleu.edu/careers, including a cover letter of interest responsive to the prompts below, CV, statement of teaching philosophy, statement of research, and the names and contact information of three references (letters may be solicited upon submission of application).\u00a0 In your cover letter explain how you understand, embody, and would contribute to each of the following at Seattle University. Please dedicate one paragraph to each prompt: 1.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Our 5-year Strategic Plan, Reigniting Our Strategic Directions;\u00a0 2.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Our Mission as a Jesuit and Catholic University; 3.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Our commitment to DEI and Inclusive Academic Excellence; 4.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Our focus on pedagogy, scholarship, service, and institution building; and\u00a0 5.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Our faculty members\u2019 role as Teacher-Scholars. Open until filled. Application review will begin June 1, 2026 and continues until the position is filled. For additional information, contact Dr. Manivong Ratts (vong@seattleu.edu), Search Committee Chair for Clinical Mental Health Counseling. \u00a0 Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: $0.00 - $0.00 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Dean-for-Student-Affairs_JR100381": {"title": "Assistant Dean for Student Affairs", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Position Description: The Assistant Dean for Student Affairs will provide forward-looking, strategic leadership to enhance the academic experience of all undergraduate and graduate students in the College of Arts and Sci ences (CAS). The Assistant Dean will oversee the college\u2019s student support services and carry ou t o ther innovative services to support the academic success, persistence, and development of stude n ts in CAS. More broadly, this position will contribute to the College\u2019s work on the University Strategic Directions and the College of Arts & Sciences strategic plan to improve our inclusive academic excellence. The Assistant Dean will provide strategic leadership to enhance the recruitment, retention, and lifecycle events of CAS students. The Assistant Dean will carry out strategies, projects, and events across the student experience, from admissions to graduation and beyond. Central responsibilities of this position include increasing and sustaining student enrollment through thoughtful recruitment and yield activities, providing orientation programs for new students and their families, and facilitating lifecycle events and student ceremonies. More broadly, this position will contribute to the University\u2019s Strategic Directions and the College of Arts & Sciences strategic plan to improve our inclusive academic excellence. Essential Job Functions Key functions of the role include providing academic and personal guidance to students, consultation to academic department chairs and all CAS faculty, representing the college in committees, board, and policy work, overseeing the academic standing review of CAS students and determining quarterly probation and dismissal decisions, and overseeing student petitions and college decisions regarding degree requirements, registration, and grading. These functions are carried out in consultation and/or in collaboration with the academic departments and programs in CAS, the CAS Dean and Dean\u2019s office, University Advising, Student Financial Services, Office of the Registrar, University Advancement, University Events, Student Affairs, and other SU colleges and offices. The Assistant Dean reports to the Dean of the College of Arts and Sciences. \u00a0 Essential duties include: Represent CAS as Dean\u2019s Designee with the Office of the Registrar on academic forms including Petitions to the Dean and Petitions for Exceptions to Policy, Hardship Withdrawals, Student Leave of Absences, and other relevant review and approval capacities. Oversee quarterly academic standings of all CAS students below the minimum required GPA, making determinations on academic warning, academic probation, and academic suspensions. The Assistant Dean will meet with students on academic suspension and will make determinations on academic reinstatement. Meet with students experiencing academic and personal hardships and assist them in navigating the Hardship Withdrawal process Represent CAS at the Dean of Students office\u2019s weekly CARE team meeting and/or consult with CAS Lead Advisor, reviewing student issues deemed moderate or higher, and planning for appropriate interventions with impacted CAS students. Maintain professional relationships with University Advising and the professional academic advisors in CAS to implement university-wide advising initiatives, polices, and procedures in CAS. Act as primary college contact for university-wide retention efforts and collaborate with Student Affairs, the Provost\u2019s Office, and others. Serve as a primary liaison to University Admissions, regularly providing updates and presentations to University Admissions staff and leadership, and partnering to plan and execute admissions strategies to recruit and yield new CAS undergraduate students. Lead CAS presence at signature University Admissions events, including Fall Preview and Admitted Students Days. Assistant Dean will recruit and coordinate participation of faculty, staff, and students and design the CAS-specific programming. Design and lead additional efforts to augment the recruitment and admissions yield strategies for CAS, including collaboration with the CAS Director of Marketing and Communication, and Seattle University Marketing Communications. Administer CAS Continuing Student Scholarships, including chairing student awards and scholarships review committee and leveraging the Scholarship Universe information system to promote scholarships, facilitate the review of applications, identifying recipients, and disbursing awards. Plan CAS Convening and Induction activities, aligning program with first-year student experience and persistence efforts, and coordinating the involvement and participation of CAS faculty, staff, and students. Administer CAS awards and recognition process, including facilitating the nomination and review process, notifying, and recognizing recipients through various methods. Plan and implement CAS Graduation Celebration, including event set-up, logistics, and coordinating CAS staff and faculty involvement. Provide oversight of Student Executive Council (UG) and Arts and Sciences Graduate Council (GR) Maintain integrity of student records and comply with university policies, deadlines, and procedures related to student enrollment. Serve on CAS and campus-wide committees and working groups. Represent CAS Dean as requested or needed. Respond strategically to evolving university priorities. Marginal Job Functions \u200bOther duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D., Ed.D., or Master\u2019s degree OR an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the position. Three to five years experience in academic advising administration. Knowledge of appropriate student development theory and a demonstrated capability for personalized and outstanding academic advising practices that contribute to the educational and personal development of students. Demonstrated professional and interpersonal characteristics needed to supervise professional, graduate, and administrative staff, providing developmental leadership and professional growth opportunities in a high-volume and high-pressure setting. Demonstrated capability for collaborative and successful working relationships with diverse stakeholders in an academic environment: faculty, department chairs, program directors, and other academic and administrative staff. Experience with university/college policies and procedures and a demonstrated ability to utilize appropriate judgment in making decisions and recommendations. Expertise in working with students at risk and knowledge of pertinent retention and persistence theory. Effective communication and other interpersonal skills, and the ability to present information in large public venues. Knowledge of program assessment, learning outcomes, and evaluation techniques. Understanding of and appreciation for the role of student services in a Jesuit institution of higher education and a commitment to the university\u2019s mission, vision, and values Demonstrated commitment to diversity and experience working with students of diverse backgrounds. Personal qualities that are essential for this position: professionalism, patience, attention to detail, follow-through, teamwork, calmness, industry, and a sense of humor. Ability to perform duties with minimal supervision. Ability to work regular evening hours and some weekends. This position requires a criminal history background check. Preferred Qualifications Experience with student data management systems Proficiency in Excel and all Microsoft office software packages Compensation at a Glance $91,468 - $93,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Garrand-Building/Assistant-Dean-for-Academic-Affairs---Student-Success--12-month-staff-position-_JR100402": {"title": "Assistant Dean for Academic Affairs & Student Success (12-month staff position)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Assistant Dean for Academic Affairs & Student Success provides leadership and oversight for academic affairs, student recruitment, progression, retention, completion, academic program quality, and student success initiatives within the College of Nursing and Health Sciences. Position Description: Seattle University invites applications to fill a new Assistant Dean for Academic Affairs & Student Success position (12-month staff). We seek an enthusiastic, dynamic, thoughtful individual whose primary responsibilities are to lead and oversee activities related to academic affairs; student recruitment, progression, retention, and completion; and compliance with student-related policies, procedures, and expectations. The Assistant Dean for Academic Affairs & Student Success must be committed to an executive leadership role that develops strategic ideas, makes organizational-level decisions, implements policies and procedures, and executes and evaluates initiatives that all contribute to student academic success and an overall positive student experience. They must be innovative, creative, collaborative, communicative, and transparent, valuing and encouraging the pursuits and contributions of a wide variety of faculty colleagues across multiple disciplines. Seattle University College of Nursing and Health Sciences seeks individuals motivated to be part of a community-engaged, creative, and dynamic learning organization committed to social justice, teaching innovation, impactful research, and the formation of health professionals endeavoring to create a more humane world by meeting the health and healthcare needs of all people, especially those marginalized and underserved. We are looking to build a team that is inspired and committed to making a difference for the greater good of society, as Seattle University has a vision to be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. The College of Nursing and Health Sciences currently houses academic programs that lead to a Bachelor of Science in Nursing, a Doctor of Nursing Practice, a Bachelor of Science in Diagnostic Ultrasound, a Bachelor of Science in Kinesiology, and a Master of Science in Kinesiology; and is positioned for growth to offer academic training programs in other health-related disciplines and professions in line with the goals of Seattle University\u2019s Reigniting Strategic Directions. Seattle University, as a whole, continues to be strengthened by an increasingly diverse campus community spanning faculty, staff, and students. Seattle University\u2019s LIFT SU, Inclusive Excellence Action Plan for Racial Equity and Antiracism, led by Seattle University\u2019s Office of Diversity and Inclusion, has fostered meaningful change to assure that people from underrepresented and historically marginalized groups feel a true sense of belonging and value. Aside from racial identity, people with lived experience across all intersectional identities and faiths or non-faiths add to the vibrancy of our campus environment and welcoming institutional climate. Primary Responsibilities Serve as principal liaison to academic affairs-related University-level units (e.g., Office of the Registrar, Office of the Provost, Graduate Studies) to represent the College\u2019s academic program and curricular interests, including the execution of academically relevant policies and procedures. Oversee the exploration and development of new academic programs, including coordinating and assisting on workflows that take ideas throughout all stages/phases of the proposal process. Collaborate with academic program leaders throughout the College, Dean\u2019s Office, and University to monitor, evaluate, and uphold academic program quality in alignment with University, College, and external accreditation and regulatory expectations and standards. Partner with campus units to organize and carry out student recruitment and admissions strategies, initiatives, and events with oversight of coordinating the College\u2019s activities and participation. Liaise with professional student advisors, faculty advisors, and University units/groups to develop and implement actions that facilitate robust student matriculation, progression, support, persistence, completion, and graduation while promoting positive student experiences and overall student welfare. Facilitate student awareness and access to programs and services focused on student success and campus life. Oversee and support student extracurricular activities and opportunities, including College-related student governance bodies, clubs, and organizations. Promote and pursue opportunities for recognizing and highlighting student and academic programming excellence. Manage and monitor, in partnership with other College administrators, student matters related to academic performance and standing, conduct, and well-being; including serving in a student advocacy role, as appropriate and when warranted. Assist other College senior administrators (e.g., Dean, Assistant Dean for Accreditation and Regulatory Compliance, academic area leads) with tasks related to monitoring, documenting, and reporting indicators for academic program health and student-related outcomes to assure compliance with accreditation standards and regulatory requirements. Minimum Qualifications Earned graduate degree, preferably in a health-related field, from an accredited higher education institution. Demonstrated successful leadership experience in academic administration in higher education. Track record of program/project development, assessment, and continuous quality improvement. Experience with program evaluation processes. Commitment to promoting excellence, diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging in an institution of higher education. Ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders, including faculty, staff, students, alumni, and external partners. Understanding of current trends, issues, and best practices in higher education. Excellent written and oral communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills. Preferred Qualifications If the applicant is a licensed healthcare professional, their license and/or certification must be current and unrestricted, and they must be eligible for licensure and/or certification in Washington state. Experience in student advising, development, and mentorship in an institution of higher education. Experience with grant writing and securing external funding for educational and training initiatives. Applicants Candidates must demonstrate a commitment to Seattle University's and the College of Nursing and Health Sciences' mission, vision, and values. The College emphasizes care of communities and populations that are underserved and marginalized. In concert with our Jesuit tradition, we are dedicated to social justice and leadership formation. Our faculty employ best practices and innovative approaches to graduating the highest quality health professionals at both the undergraduate and doctoral levels. Nationally respected health and research institutions in the Western Washington and Pacific Northwest region offer opportunities for collaboration, and Seattle University faculty, staff, and students are eager to engage in interdisciplinary endeavors. Compensation at a Glance $90,000 \u2013 $92,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Advising-Specialist_JR100253": {"title": "Advising Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Reporting to the Albers Senior Graduate Programs Advisor, this Limited Term Appointment (LTA) position provides administrative and academic specialist support to the Albers Graduate academic advisors and Albers graduate students through the transition from quarter to semester terms during the calendar unification at Seattle University. The Albers Graduate LTA advising specialist will work as part of a dynamic, student-oriented advising team, on projects related to the calendar unification project and student retention. The LTA advising specialist role requires creativity, attention to detail, and comfort learning and teaching new technology. This Limited Term Appointment (LTA) position is expected to end on June 30, 2028. Position Description: Support Academic Planning through Semester Transition (40%) Assist with the review and creation of quarter-to-semester transition materials (excel files, curriculum flowcharts, sample degree plans, course translation guides, etc.) Publish and maintain files in Albers SharePoint intranet site. Accurately track student progress through Excel, Program Evaluations, and Bower BI tools. Support creation and revision of academic plans for Albers graduate students that integrate their completed courses on the quarter system with their future required courses in the semester system to ensure degree requirements are met for an on-time graduation. Investigate and understand the degree requirements under both quarter and semester calendars for the degrees represented in the Albers graduate programs. Provide notes to inform Albers graduate advisors of decisions and questions for their review with students. Learn and operate the degree planning software in both Colleague and Workday. Provide technical support for Albers graduate advisors in learning the new platform (Workday). Identify potential irregularities and inaccuracies within the software implementation and/or course scheduling and report to supervisor. After registration, review student schedules term by term to ensure individual academic plans are followed; flag any students who deviate from their plan for their assigned Albers graduate advisor. Student Outreach (15%) Participate in email and phone-calling campaigns and other forms of student outreach, using the Slate CRM platform. Assist Albers graduate advisors with outreach to their student caseloads for the purpose of scheduling meetings, providing information, or seeking student input. Student Programming and Communications (20%) With direction from the Albers Senior Advisor, develop and implement strategies for informing students about academic advising, and student responsibilities around these key transitions. In coordination with the Marketing and Communications office, assist to create informative content for , internal and external websites, and print materials. Assist with the coordination and organization of events for students, including Registration workshops and Academic Planning /Calendar Unification information sessions. Coordinate logistical arrangements for events, meetings and workshops, including room reservations, setup, electronic registrations, tech support needs, and catering. Administrative support (25%) Support Albers Grad Advising in responding to student inquiries. Inform students on academic forms, processes, procedures, and resources related to SU and the school/college. Support scheduling Albers graduate advisor-student appointments. Assist with the coordination and dissemination of information to support student success. Outreach to students who are required to meet with an advisor for Education Plan approval, Academic Standing, and other circumstances. Other Duties as Assigned Respond to changing needs and tasks with flexibility and creativity. Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $55,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Associate-Director--Employer-and-Experiential-Partnerships_JR100384": {"title": "Associate Director, Employer and Experiential Partnerships", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Associate Director provides leadership for the development, management, and stewardship of employer and industry partnerships that expand access to internships, employment, experiential learning, mentorship, recruiting programs, and other career-connected opportunities for Seattle University students and recent alumni. Reporting to the Executive Director of Career Engagement, the Associate Director translates the university\u2019s institutional employer engagement strategy into coordinated outreach, partnership development, recruiting activity, and measurable student opportunities. The position manages the full lifecycle of employer engagement from prospect identification and relationship cultivation through activation, stewardship, assessment, and renewal. The Associate Director works closely with Career Engagement colleagues, the Albers Career Center, faculty, experiential learning partners, Alumni Engagement, Advancement, Student Employment, and other campus stakeholders to support an integrated university-wide career ecosystem. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to Seattle University\u2019s Jesuit Catholic mission, professional formation, social justice, equity, inclusion, cultural humility, and student success. Position Description: The Associate Director provides leadership for the development, management, and stewardship of employer and industry partnerships that expand access to internships, employment, experiential learning, mentorship, recruiting programs, and other career-connected opportunities for Seattle University students and recent alumni. Reporting to the Executive Director of Career Engagement, the Associate Director translates the university\u2019s institutional employer engagement strategy into coordinated outreach, partnership development, recruiting activity, and measurable student opportunities. The position manages the full lifecycle of employer engagement from prospect identification and relationship cultivation through activation, stewardship, assessment, and renewal. The Associate Director works closely with Career Engagement colleagues, the Albers Career Center, faculty, experiential learning partners, Alumni Engagement, Advancement, Student Employment, and other campus stakeholders to support an integrated university-wide career ecosystem. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to Seattle University\u2019s Jesuit Catholic mission, professional formation, social justice, equity, inclusion, cultural humility, and student success. Essential Functions Employer and Industry Partnership Development - 35% Develop and maintain a diverse portfolio of employer, industry, government, nonprofit, and community partnerships aligned with student interests, academic programs, workforce trends, and institutional priorities. Identify and cultivate new employers that can provide internships, entry-level employment, project-based learning, mentorship, job-shadowing, career treks, classroom engagement, and other experiential opportunities. Build employer pipelines across industries and occupational areas, with particular attention to sectors with strong regional demand and relevance to Seattle University students. Meet with employers to understand workforce needs and develop customized engagement strategies that connect organizations with Seattle University students, faculty, academic programs, and university initiatives. Advise employers on recruiting practices, internship program development, employer branding, campus visibility, and inclusive approaches to attracting and retaining early-career talent. Proactively cultivate organizations committed to creating equitable access to employment and experiential learning for first-generation students, students of color, students with disabilities, international students, veterans, and other historically underrepresented populations. Maintain awareness of labor-market conditions, emerging industries, workforce trends, recruiting practices, and changing employer expectations. Represent Seattle University and Career Engagement at employer meetings, professional associations, chambers of commerce, workforce-development conferences, and community events. Employer Stewardship and Partnership Activation - 20% Manage the full employer relationship lifecycle, including cultivation, onboarding, activation, stewardship, assessment, renewal, and expansion. Develop engagement plans for priority employers and ensure consistent communication and follow-through across the university. Translate employer interest into specific opportunities such as internships, jobs, mentoring relationships, sponsored projects, career events, classroom presentations, site visits, and experiential learning partnerships. Coordinate with academic colleges and campus partners to connect employer opportunities with appropriate students, programs, faculty, and student organizations. Support college-based career and professional formation leaders by providing access to employer relationships, labor-market insights, and external partnership opportunities. Strengthen employer retention by providing responsive service, gathering feedback, communicating impact, and identifying opportunities for deeper engagement. Develop resources and engagement pathways that make it easier for employers to understand how to connect with Seattle University. Recruiting and Career-Connected Programming - 25% Provide strategic and operational leadership for employer-facing recruiting programs and career-connected events. Collaborate with the Career Education team to ensure that employer programming complements students\u2019 career readiness preparation and learning needs. Partner with the Albers Career Center and other university offices to coordinate employer outreach, reduce duplication, and create a consistent experience for employer partners. Recruit employers and industry professionals to participate in mock interviews, r\u00e9sum\u00e9 reviews, mentorship programs, classroom presentations, career education workshops, and other student-learning activities. Support the development of micro-internships, project-based experiences, job-shadowing, and other lower-barrier opportunities for students who are still developing professional experience. Ensure that employer programs and recruiting activities comply with university policies, applicable employment regulations, ethical recruiting standards, and National Association of Colleges and Employers guidance. Institutional Collaboration and Experiential Learning - 10% Serve as an employer-facing resource for university initiatives related to internships, experiential learning, mentorship, workforce development, AI career readiness, and professional formation. Help identify gaps in experiential opportunity by academic program, student population, career interest, and industry. Support the development of scalable practices for connecting academic learning with employer-sponsored projects, internships, research, service, and other applied experiences. Participate in cross-campus committees and working groups related to employer engagement, experiential learning, student outcomes, and workforce readiness. Ensure employer engagement efforts complement specialized career services within the Albers School of Business and avoid unnecessary duplication. Data, Systems, Assessment, and Reporting - 10% Establish measurable goals for employer outreach, partnership development, opportunity creation, employer retention, and student engagement. Maintain accurate employer relationship, recruiting, and opportunity data within Handshake and other approved university systems. Develop dashboards, reports, and summaries that communicate employer engagement activity and impact to Career Engagement leadership and university stakeholders. Assess employer satisfaction and use feedback to continuously improve services, programs, and partnership practices. Monitor internships, jobs, recruiting activity, employer participation, and other indicators of partnership effectiveness. Collaborate with Career Engagement colleagues to connect employer engagement data with student participation and post-graduation outcomes. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in business, higher education, human resources, communications, marketing, public relations, nonprofit leadership, or a related field, or equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience. At least five years of progressively responsible experience in employer relations, university recruiting, talent acquisition, business development, workforce development, corporate relations, alumni relations, career services, or a related field. Experience translating relationships into programs, opportunities, partnerships, or measurable outcomes. Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, presentation, and relationship-management skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple partnerships, projects, events, deadlines, and priorities. Ability to work collaboratively across organizational boundaries and with stakeholders who have varied goals and responsibilities. Demonstrated commitment to equity, inclusion, social justice, cultural humility, and serving diverse student populations. Ability to use data and technology to manage relationships, assess performance, and inform decisions. Availability for occasional evening or weekend programs and local travel to employer and community sites. Preferred Qualifications Experience in a college or university career center, employer-relations operation, talent acquisition function, workforce-development organization, or corporate partnership role. Knowledge of university recruiting, internship development, experiential learning, mentorship, early-career hiring, and employer engagement practices. Familiarity with customer relationship management systems, career-services technology, or employer relationship-management platforms. Experience using labor-market information and employer data to guide strategy. Familiarity with National Association of Colleges and Employers standards, career-readiness competencies, and ethical recruiting principles. Understanding of the Seattle and Puget Sound employer, nonprofit, government, and workforce-development landscape. Experience supporting fundraising, sponsorship, corporate engagement, or partnership models that include financial and nonfinancial investment. Seattle University Mission Alignment The Associate Director advances Seattle University\u2019s mission of educating the whole person, promoting professional formation, and empowering leaders for a just and humane world. By connecting students with meaningful work, professional relationships, experiential learning, and organizations committed to positive social and economic impact, the position helps students translate their education into lives of purpose, leadership, and service. The Associate Director will ensure that employer engagement reflects Seattle University\u2019s Jesuit values and expands access to social capital, professional networks, and career opportunities for all students. Compensation at a Glance $67,000 - $74,700 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director--Orientation-Programs_JR100372": {"title": "Director, Orientation Programs", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Director of Orientation Programs serves as the institutional leader for visioning, strategic direction, and delivery of new student orientation and transition support initiatives for all incoming undergraduate first year and transfer students across the First Hill and South Lake Union campuses. This includes attention to support of traditionally underrepresented student populations and an expansive understanding of the unique onboarding needs of each school or college\u2019s programs at both campus locations. The Director leads the university\u2019s efforts to continuously strengthen and align the path from deposit to matriculation. Position Description: The Director of Orientation Programs serves as the institutional leader for visioning, strategic direction, and delivery of new student orientation and transition support initiatives for all incoming undergraduate first year and transfer students across the First Hill and South Lake Union campuses. This includes attention to support of traditionally underrepresented student populations and an expansive understanding of the unique onboarding needs of each school or college\u2019s programs at both campus locations. \u00a0The Director leads the university\u2019s efforts to continuously s trengthen and align the path from deposit to matriculation. The Director is expected to function in a collaborative manner with a high degree of visibility and contact with students, families, staff, faculty, and administration. This role requires innovation and knowledge of leading-edge practices to enhance the experience of current student populations.\u00a0 In alignment with the university\u2019s commitment to student success, retention, and persistence, the director will use a framework of social justice and Jesuit education to deliver a comprehensive entry-year experience and celebration of key milestones. Essential Functions: Strategic Leadership and Vision: 40% Lead university-wide strategy for new student onboarding in alignment with Seattle University\u2019s mission and institutional priorities. Assume responsibility for incoming students post-deposit up to and through the start of their first term. Design, implement, and assess in-person and online new student transition curriculum. Analyze and prepare data and metrics reports and presentations for campus partners and senior leadership in regular cadence leading up to start of each term. Strategize and deliver proactive and reactive responses to student progress through New Student Checklist tasks, with emphasis on high melt risk students. Craft and implement individualized support for unique populations, including but not limited to: Adult learners, BIPOC students, Commuter students, Exchange students, First Generation students, Honors students, International students, LGBTQ+ students, Scholar cohorts, Student Athletes, Transfer students, and Veteran and Military-Connected students. Maintain knowledge of unique onboarding needs for Cornish College of the Arts student experience. Implement behavior-driven anti-melt student and family communications plan to drive completion of essential onboarding tasks on New Student Checklist. Develop strategic plans, goals, and measurable objectives for Orientation Programs. Lead regular new student cohort check-in meetings and deliverables in collaboration with Admissions, Housing and Residence Life, and University Advising. Lead strategic collaborations with 35+ university departments to collectively deliver a seamless orientation experience Regularly conduct benchmarking with peer institutions and review research and leading-edge work in the field to evaluate for utility at Seattle University. Program and Event Coordination: 35% Manage 20+ university-wide in-person orientation events annually including one-day, multi-day, overnight, and weeklong experiences Oversee daily program and event operations including schedule, content, space reservation/setup, catering, parking/transportation, equipment rentals, campus access, contracts, staffing, technology, and more. Coordinate parent, family, and supporter onboarding, including virtual/in-person events and communications in collaboration with Parent & Family Engagement Manage design, operation, and enhancements of Orientation Portal in Slate CRM, centralizing New Student Checklist, Advising & Registration, event registration, and communications Serve as lead event coordinator for New Student Convocation in collaboration with Office of the Provost. Coordinate Early Bird Immersion early arrival programs in collaboration with relevant campus partners, supporting event operations, registrations, and scholarships Co-lead Move-In Experience Committee with staff in Housing & Residence Life Serve as university administrator for ALEKS Math Placement Assessment Design new student resource guides and online learning modules to support incoming student and family learning and preparedness. Co-coordinate advising and registration processes for incoming students in collaboration with University Advising. Collaborate on Common Text program with University Core and First-Year Academic Engagement. Partner with Marketing & Communication on digital and print design assets in alignment with university branding Establish and continuously enhance strategic integration of faculty and academic affairs across orientation programming and services. Departmental Leadership: 25% Recruit, hire, train, supervise, and evaluate staff, including one full-time administrative coordinator, two part-time graduate assistants, and 45+ undergraduate orientation leaders. Conduct assessment, evaluation, and data reporting of departmental programs. Develop multi-year, sustainable financial plans and manage departmental budget to align. Maintain breadth and depth of knowledge of operational processes across First Hill and South Lake Union campus to support event management and new student onboarding. Serve as primary speaker for all orientation events. Benchmark, calculate, and manage revenue related to orientation fees, guest fees, and other related program fees. Serve as a member of Student Success Leadership Committee. Oversee departmental web presence (website, social media) and Orientation (Guidebook) app. Promote a campus culture of shared responsibility for new student onboarding. Educate campus partners on research and best practices and collaboratively implement in a way fitting to Seattle University. Establish and sustain sponsor partnerships with community businesses. Support campus partner events through staffing presence and co-hosting programs and services. Marginal Job Functions Participate in divisional and campus committees. Represent the division and the university to external constituents, including at professional associations. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Master\u2019s degree in higher education and five years of progressive experience in student affairs with skills in administering and educational program or a related field required OR an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to perform the essential functions of the job as listed above. A demonstrated commitment to inclusion and social justice in alignment with the University's mission, vision and values Effective skills in building and sustaining campus relationships and working collaboratively. Demonstrated supervisory and/or mentorship skills with students. Effective communication, interpersonal and team-building skills. Ability to work effectively one-on-one with students, staff, and faculty. An understanding of and appreciation for student development and services in a Jesuit institution of higher education. A willingness to work flexible hours including evenings and weekends. This position requires a criminal history background check. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in higher education or related field and seven years working with orientation and/or transition programs. Demonstrated ability to successfully navigate the diverse interests of multiple campus stakeholders. Demonstrated leadership and staff supervisory experience. Knowledge and experience of specialized student populations listed above. Excellent verbal, written and organizational skills; capability to multi-task; demonstrated ability to manage budgets, develop and implement assessment; and strategic planning methods and reporting functions. Skilled use of technology, social media, and/or managing communication campaigns. Prior experience and training using CRM platforms. Compensation at a Glance $88,000 - $92,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Admissions-Evaluator---International-Credential-Evaluations_JR100271": {"title": "Admissions Evaluator - International Credential Evaluations", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Admissions Evaluator \u2013 International Credential Evaluations carefully evaluates international transcripts for GPA calculations, transferable credit and major prerequisites. The Evaluator serves as a Designated School Official (DSO) and issues I-20's for international students to be able to apply for visas. As the subject matter expert in international evaluations, the Evaluator also plays a key role in functions related to admissions operations for all undergraduate applicants as part of a team of Admissions Evaluators, which includes evaluating transcripts and calculating GPA\u2019s for domestic students, and maintaining data and applicant/record cleanliness. Position Description: International (60%) Serves as the subject matter expert for evaluating secondary and post-secondary international credentials for incoming students. This includes calculating GPAs according to grading scales from other countries, determining course equivalencies from post-secondary institutions outside the United States, and applying applicants\u2019 previous coursework towards Seattle University\u2019s degree requirements. Regularly reviews other components of international applications, including auditing for fulfillment of the university\u2019s English Proficiency requirement and confirming citizenship information. Serves as a Designated School Official (DSO), which involves issuing initial I-20's for incoming international students. Works in close partnership with the International Student and Scholar Center and participates in regular DSO trainings. Communicates with students on missing materials needed for I-20 issuance. Serves as a member of the undergraduate international admissions group which includes the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment and the International Transfer Admissions Counselor. Participates in regular meetings with the group and supports international functions in close collaboration with the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment. Supports with monitoring emails in the general international inbox and responding to student questions as needed. General Admissions Operations (35%) Determines course equivalencies and applies applicants\u2019 previous coursework towards Seattle University\u2019s degree requirements and posts appropriate equivalencies of transferred credit hours awarded and current class standing in the University\u2019s ERP and CRM systems, specifically Colleague, Workday, and Slate. Serves as an academic policy resource for incoming freshmen and transfer students regarding University Core and major transfer requirements for all undergraduate degrees and is a key contact for Transfer Admissions Counselors and designated academic liaisons throughout the University on academic policy matters related to the onboarding of undergraduate students with transferrable credit. Adds course equivalencies to the online TES database system and assists in the development of effective tools/resources for best practices in communicating transfer course equivalencies to prospective transfers. Assists in maintaining data and applicant/record cleanliness in various ways working closely with other individuals within the Admissions Operations team and Research and Analytics. These tasks may consist of editing small parts of multiple records, general audits, the scanning and assigning of documents, exporting of data for mass corrections, etc. Other (5%) Participates in office-wide committees, projects, and other duties as assigned in Undergraduate Admissions. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree . Demonstrated commitment university's mission, vision and values. One or more years of experience in detailed data entry. Ability to effectively use and quickly learn new technological systems. Strong critical thinking skills and the ability to exercise professional judgment to make informed decisions based on experience, reason and policy while considering issues of equity, fairness and historical precedent Adept at managing and prioritizing multiple tasks Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment. The ideal candidate will be a resilient and innovative problem solver, have excellent organizational abilities with acute attention to detail, and strong interpersonal skills that enable him/her to develop positive relationships with key campus partners and provide personalized attention to prospective students throughout the admissions process Preferred Prior experience in an educational environment in the areas of credential evaluation, academic advising, and/or transfer evaluations. Familiarity with international education and exposure to schooling systems in other countries. Compensation at a Glance $47,000 - $49,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Executive-Coordinator_JR100368": {"title": "Executive Coordinator", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Executive Coordinator provides high-level administrative and operational support to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering, contributing to the effective leadership and advancement of the College. This position supports the Dean\u2019s efforts to promote and represent the College within the University and with external constituents, including donors, alumni, corporate partners, prospective students, and other key stakeholders. The Executive Coordinator manages administrative functions within the Dean\u2019s Office and supports special projects and initiatives as directed by the Dean. The position also serves as a key resource to faculty and staff, providing guidance and information related to University and College policies, procedures, rank and tenure processes, and business operations. The Executive Coordinator reports directly to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering and plays an important role in fostering effective communication, coordination, and collaboration across the College. Position Description: This position supports the efforts of the dean to lead and promote the college both within the university and externally to donors, alumni, corporate partners, and prospective students, etc. The executive coordinator is responsible for administrative support duties in the Dean's Office and supports various projects as the dean requires. This position serves as a resource for faculty and staff regarding policies, procedures, rank and tenure, and the business functions of the college. This position reports to the Dean of the College of Science and Engineering. \u00a0 Essential Job Functions Executive Assistant (45%) \u00a0 Schedule the dean's meetings with faculty, staff, other members of the university community, donors, and visitors. \u00a0 Take minutes and transcribe same for Executive Committee meetings as well as college-wide meetings, as needed. \u00a0 Make travel arrangements for the dean. Communicate with the appropriate offices on campus to fulfill the dean's requests for information.\u202f \u00a0 Collaborate with college\u2019s development officer to set up donor meetings for the dean, special visits, and events. \u00a0 Serve as contact for requests for college calendar postings. Oversee permissions for college listservs. \u00a0 Provide administrative support for the Executive Committee, Student Advisory Council, faculty searches , rank & tenure process, annual performance reviews, sabbaticals, and other activities designated by the dean. \u00a0 \u00a0 Event Planner (20%) \u00a0 Arrange faculty/staff meetings, events and all college-wide receptions and gatherings. \u00a0 Create agendas, schedules, orders of events and briefs for events as needed. \u00a0 Collaborate with other college and university principals as needed for event planning. \u00a0 Create and maintain invitations, name tags and RSVP lists. \u00a0 Coordinate with CES, Catering and ITCS as needed.\u202f \u00a0 Clerical (15%) \u00a0 Keep track of forms requiring dean's signature and route them accordingly. \u00a0 Maintain/update personnel files of faculty members and college roster updates. \u00a0 Coordinate rank-and-tenure dossier submission and support rank -and-tenure process within the college. \u00a0 Produce grade distribution reports for faculty members seeking promotion and/or tenure. \u00a0 Handle paperwork for annual performance reviews, sabbatical requests, and junior faculty development leaves .\u202f \u00a0 Reporting (10%) \u00a0 Using university systems, collect data and produce various reports, including, but not limited to: enrollment trends, student credit hours generated, and other measures of the productivity of individual departments and the entire college. \u00a0 Prepare presentations for dean.\u202f \u00a0 Office Reception (10%) \u00a0 Receive and direct incoming phone calls, greet visitors, and provide visitors and callers with general information. \u00a0 Route mail and packages. Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $62,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Part-time-Jewish-Chaplain_JR100369": {"title": "Part-time Jewish Chaplain", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Responsible for providing pastoral care, leading weekly student programs, stewarding program budgets, cultivating strategic partnerships with local organizations, and coordinating interfaith initiatives to strengthen Jewish life and presence on campus. This is a part-time position scheduled for 10 hours per week, 11 months per year, with July off. Essential Job Functions Accompaniment and spiritual support of Jewish students primarily, with some overlap with supporting staff and faculty (40%) Work with Jewish Student Union and other Jewish student leaders to develop religious services and gatherings on campus (20%) Oversee and develop inventory and administration of current Jewish prayer spaces (10%) Participate, collaborate and engage in inter-religious programing in partnership with University Ministry, Theology and Religious Studies, Mission Integration and the Center for Ecumenical and Interreligious Engagement (10%) Provide spiritual leadership and accompaniment of all students with a spirit of spiritual and religious inclusivity\u00a0 and educate the community about Judaism (10%) Attend team and division meetings where appropriate and engage in necessary administrative tasks such as room scheduling, maintaining budget for programs and working with marketing to share and communicate offerings. (10%) Qualifications Designation as Rabbi by a Jewish religious authority An MDiv\u00a0master\u2019s degree in Jewish studies or related At least 2-3 years of experience in a university setting Familiarity and passion around Jesuit educational values Compensation at a Glance Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. $35 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Part-Time-Muslim-Chaplin_JR100367": {"title": "Part-Time Muslim Chaplin", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 and 10 hours per week Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: The part-time Muslim Chaplin will provide pastoral care and support to students; lead weekly student programs; manage and steward program budgets; develop and maintain strategic partnerships with local organizations; and coordinate interfaith initiatives that foster and strengthen Muslim life and presence on campus. This is a part-time position scheduled for 10 hours per week, 11 months per year, with July off. Accompaniment and spiritual support of Muslim students primarily, with some overlap with supporting staff and faculty (40%) Work with Muslim Student Association and other Muslim student leaders to develop religious services and gatherings on campus (20%) Oversee and develop inventory and administration of current Muslim prayer spaces (10%) Participate, collaborate and engage in inter-religious programming in partnership with Campus Ministry, Theology and Religious Studies, Mission Integration and the Center for Ecumenical and Interreligious Engagement (10%) Provide spiritual leadership and accompaniment of all students with a spirit of spiritual and religious inclusivity\u00a0 and educate the community about Islam (10%) Attend team and division meetings where appropriate and engage in necessary administrative tasks such as room scheduling, maintaining budget for programs and working with marketing to share and communicate offerings. (10% \u00a0 Experience/Qualifications Designation as an Imam by a Muslim religious authority An MDiv\u00a0 master\u2019s degree in Islamic studies or related At least 2-3 years of experience in a university setting Familiarity and passion around Jesuit educational values Compensation at a Glance Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. $35 per hour Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100352": {"title": "Senior Administrative Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Seattle University is seeking an experienced Administrative Assistant to join the Office of the Global Engagement. The Office of Global Engagement\u2019s mission is to foster international partnerships, support engagement through global grants, and move forward with the Global Jesuit Initiatives in Latin America, Asia Pacific and South Asia. \u00a0 As Sr. Administrative Assistant, you will support the work of the Associate Vice Provost for Global Engagement and the Associate Vice Provost for Global Academic Programs by scheduling and staffing meetings, coordinating projects, arranging travel for international visitors, preparing reports and correspondence, and managing files. The ideal candidate is a dynamic professional who can handle confidential matters with professionalism, work with a positive and solutions-oriented mindset, and demonstrate strong judgment, tact, and diplomacy. Support for the Associate Vice Provosts (50%) Manage calendars and incoming external calls for the Associate Vice Provosts, ensuring sufficient work time and time for meetings and events. Coordinate travel arrangements and itineraries. Assist with emails as directed. Communicate with internal and external constituents, drafting responses to correspondence, where appropriate. Submit, track, and reconcile expenditures. Process invoices, requisitions, reimbursements, and payments for the office. Work in partnership with the Provost, Senior Vice Provosts, Vice Provosts, and Deans, and their office staff on all business needs. Support for the Office of Global Engagement (30%) Manage meeting and event logistics, including distribution of materials, room reservations, A/V, technical support, and catering. Prepare meeting minutes.\u00a0 Coordinate travel arrangements and itineraries for visiting international scholars and guests. Maintain email distribution lists and partnership databases.\u00a0 Prepare correspondence, newsletters, the annual report, and other documents. Manage the Office of Global Engagement files and SharePoint website.\u00a0 Support the Global Jesuit Initiatives as directed by the Special Assistant to the Provost for Strategic Initiatives. Support for the Center for Language and International Communication (CLIC) (20%) Submit, track, and reconcile expenditures. Process invoices, requisitions, reimbursements, and payments for the office. Maintain CLIC alumni database and photo archives. Coordinate logistics for the CLIC Language Partner Program. Assist with Global Engagement grant-funded activities (e.g., career workshops) in partnership with campus offices. Critical Success Factors Confidentiality, diplomacy, tact, discretion, and collaboration are required. Courteous and efficient handling of all contacts. Diplomacy in responding to domestic and international visitors, phone calls, and customer service issues.\u00a0 Willingness to receive work from executive staff and senior level administrators, often with little or no instructions or established practices or procedures.\u00a0 Comfort working independently and making judicious decisions based on thorough knowledge of functional area policies and procedures.\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job. 2-3 years of experience providing successful administrative support. Demonstrated ability to multi-task and prioritize projects. Strong organizational and planning skills. Excellent verbal and written communication skills required including experience preparing, reviewing, and editing correspondence and documents. Advanced knowledge of all Microsoft Office products.\u00a0 Quickly learn new systems and adapt to existing practices, while also looking for efficiencies in existing processes. Reliability. Attention to detail. Positive attitude and willingness to pitch in as needed. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree.\u00a0 Experience supporting a senior executive. Knowledge and experience in higher education. Knowledge and experience in international relations.\u00a0 Thorough knowledge of a university\u2019s organization and operation. Compensation at a Glance Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. $42,900 - $52,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director-Web-Strategy-and-Development_JR100360": {"title": "Director Web Strategy and Development", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Position Description: Primary Responsibilities Digital Strategy & Product Leadership Develop and execute a comprehensive, multi-year web and digital experience strategy aligned with Seattle University's strategic and marketing priorities. Lead major website redesigns, platform migrations, integrations, and digital transformation initiatives. Partner with university leadership and stakeholders to identify opportunities for digital experiences to improve recruitment, enrollment, engagement, fundraising, and institutional reputation. Establish scalable approaches for integrating newly acquired, affiliated, or evolving institutional web properties into the broader Seattle University digital ecosystem. Web Platforms & Technical Strategy Provide strategic and technical leadership for the University's web technology ecosystem, including Terminalfour, WordPress, Drupal, SharePoint. Establish platform architecture, configuration, development standards, release processes, integrations, APIs, and technical governance. Evaluate the long-term role of existing platforms and recommend consolidation, migration, modernization, or replacement when appropriate. Partner with ITS on infrastructure, authentication, integrations, security, cloud services, data architecture, and enterprise technology standards. Oversee development environments, source control, Git workflows, deployment processes, CDNs, performance optimization, monitoring, and quality assurance. Ensure web platforms remain scalable, maintainable, secure, performant, and resilient. User Experience, Content Strategy & Search Engine Optimization Lead user experience, information architecture, content strategy, navigation, search, SEO, and digital discovery. Improve audience journeys, conversion, engagement, findability, and task completion through research and continuous optimization. Establish scalable content models, structured data, taxonomy, and SEO practices supporting both traditional and AI-powered discovery. Establish technical and content SEO standards across institutional websites. Optimize University content for traditional search engines as well as evolving AI-powered search, answer engines, and generative discovery experiences. Oversee structured data, metadata, taxonomy, schema, content relationships, and other technologies that improve discoverability. Analytics, Measurement & Optimization Establish a comprehensive digital measurement strategy for university web properties. Oversee implementation and governance of web analytics, tag management, dashboards, conversion tracking, and related measurement technologies. Develop reporting systems that connect website activity to institutional outcomes including recruitment, enrollment, engagement, and conversion. Use quantitative and qualitative data to prioritize development and content improvements. Establish key performance indicators for digital platforms and communicate performance, opportunities, and risks to university leadership. Accessibility, Privacy, Security & Governance Ensure University digital properties meet applicable WCAG, ADA, privacy, security, and regulatory requirements. Establish University-wide web governance, publishing, accessibility, quality assurance, and development standards. Maintain processes supporting emergency communications, monitoring, incident response, and business continuity. Provide training, documentation, and support for the University's community of web editors. University Partnerships & Vendor Management Serve as a strategic advisor to university leadership, schools, colleges, departments, and administrative units on web strategy, technology, digital experience, and emerging technologies. Lead relationships with external digital agencies, development partners, consultants, hosting providers, SaaS vendors, and technology partners. Manage contracts, scopes of work, budgets, renewals, vendor performance, and technology investments. Coordinate multiple internal and external teams working across the University's digital ecosystem. Partner closely with ITS, Enrollment Management, Graduate Studies, Advancement, Academic Affairs, and other university stakeholders. Leadership & Partnership Lead a talented multidisciplinary team spanning web development, content strategy, web support, UX/UI, visual design, and student employees. Manage external agencies, consultants, technology vendors, contracts, budgets, and strategic partnerships. Collaborate across Marketing, ITS, Enrollment Management, Graduate Studies, Advancement, Academic Affairs, schools, and colleges. Advocate appropriate staffing, technology, budget, and resources necessary to support the University's evolving digital ecosystem. Emerging Technology Develop and lead a responsible strategy for incorporating artificial intelligence and agentic technologies into the University's web and digital operations. Identify opportunities for AI-powered search, conversational interfaces, intelligent content discovery, personalization, recommendation systems, and audience experiences across university websites. Partner with ITS and university leadership to ensure emerging technologies are adopted responsibly and in accordance with institutional policies and risk-management standards. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in an applicable field of study or equivalent combination of education and significant professional experience. 7+ years of progressive experience in web, digital technology, digital experience, or related fields, including significant leadership responsibility. Demonstrated experience developing and executing enterprise web and digital strategies. Experience managing complex, multi-site and multi-platform web ecosystems. Experience with enterprise content management and digital experience platforms such as Terminalfour, Hannon Hill\u2019s Cascade Server, WordPress, Drupal, and SharePoint. Experience leading large-scale website redesigns, migrations, platform implementations, and digital transformation initiatives. Strong understanding of modern web architecture, APIs, integrations, cloud platforms, CDNs, Git-based development, CI/CD, and deployment workflows. Strong knowledge of front-end web technologies including HTML, CSS, and JavaScript and familiarity with modern development frameworks and practices. Experience with web analytics, tag management, SEO, structured data, conversion measurement, and digital performance reporting. Strong understanding of WCAG accessibility requirements, privacy, security, responsive design, UX/UI, and web governance. Demonstrated ability to manage vendors, agencies, contracts, budgets, and complex cross-functional initiatives. Demonstrated experience directly managing and developing a multidisciplinary digital team (for example, web development, content strategy, and UX/design), including budget and vendor oversight. Excellent project and product management skills with the ability to manage multiple strategic priorities and cross-functional initiatives simultaneously. Excellent interpersonal, communication, and relationship-building skills, with demonstrated ability to influence and collaborate across technical teams, marketers, content creators, senior leadership, and distributed stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to Seattle University's mission, vision, values, and commitment to inclusive excellence. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in web technology, digital strategy, computer science, information systems, communications, marketing technology, or a related discipline. More than 10 years of experience leading web and digital technology programs. Experience leading web and digital technology programs at a large, decentralized, multi-brand, or multi-entity institution or organization. Higher education digital strategy or technology leadership experience. Experience evaluating, consolidating, or migrating enterprise CMS/DXP platforms across a large, multi-site ecosystem. Experience with enterprise search platforms, structured content, personalization, marketing technology, and CRM integrations. Experience developing governance frameworks for artificial intelligence and other emerging digital technologies, and translating them into practical, scalable programs that advance institutional goals. Experience leading larger, more complex multidisciplinary digital teams spanning development, content strategy, UX, analytics, and digital operations, including external partners and agencies. \u00a0 Compensation at a Glance $120,000 - $130,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Admissions-Counselor_JR100481": {"title": "Admissions Counselor", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Admissions Counselor will join a dynamic undergraduate admissions team that represents Seattle University's Jesuit academic and co-curricular opportunities to prospective students, their families and supporters, and the high school college counselor community. This position works primarily with first-time-in-college (FTIC) students applying directly from high school. However, the counseling team supports all prospective students, including First-Year, Transfer, Arts, Pre-College, and Post-Baccalaureate, by providing advising and support through email and phone communication, appointments, events on and off campus, and other outreach programs as needed. Position Description: Recruitment Management - 45% Provide information about the University's academic opportunities to prospective students, families, counselors, advisors, and other stakeholders through presentations, emails, calls, and one-on-one conversation. Plan, develop, and implement on and off campus recruitment travel and activities primarily in assigned geographic regions. Meet with prospective students through various channels, including high school visits, campus interviews, information sessions, and workshops. Act as a connection point between students and other campus stakeholders (i.e. faculty, financial aid, registrar, etc.) to meet undergraduate enrollment goals. Engage in efforts to make higher education more accessible, equitable, and inclusive; participate in working groups to embed these efforts in job responsibilities. Approximately 10 to 12 weeks of in state and out of state travel. Application Review and Recommendations - 25% Review, evaluate, and provide initial undergraduate admission recommendations based on university criteria and artistic standards (where necessary) Assist students in submitting materials to complete their applications and advise through questions about best-fit majors, changing majors, and updating start terms. Event Attendance - 15% Support and actively participate in programs and events engaging prospective First Year, Transfer, Post Baccalaureate, and Pre College students, their families, community organizations, college counselors, and advisors. Attend major admission events such as preview days and admitted student days. Evening and weekend work required, especially in fall and spring. Other Duties - 15% Manage and work on assigned project areas throughout the year. Other duties as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Education Requirements Minimum: Bachelor's Degree (4-year) in a relevant field. Experience Minimum of 1 year of professional work experience, or a combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the duties and responsibilities of the job. Minimum Qualifications Must possess well-developed organizational skills. Confidence in public speaking to both large and small groups. Strong oral and written communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain cooperative and effective working relationships with others. A commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion, and supporting diverse student populations. Ability and willingness to travel and work evenings and weekends as necessary. Willingness to learn quickly, ask questions, and accept constructive feedback. Valid driver's license required. Preferred Qualifications Speaks, writes, and reads one of the following languages: Spanish, Vietnamese, Somali, Mandarin, Tagalog, Amharic, or Tigrinya, and is interested in utilizing language skills to serve prospective students and families in the college search process. Knowledge of Microsoft Office products and federal and state privacy laws. Experience using an ERP and CRM, specifically Workday and Technolutions Slate CRM. Knowledge of financial aid trends and issues. Experience working with diverse young adult and youth populations. Physical Demands This position performs work in an office environment. Frequency values: Never; Seldom (1\u201310%, 0\u20131 hour); Occasional (11\u201333%, 1\u20133 hours); Frequent (34\u201366%, 3\u20136 hours); Constant (67\u2013100%, not restricted). \u00a0 Travel - Frequent Lifting boxes or other items for travel and events - Occasional Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $53,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant_JR100376": {"title": "Senior Administrative Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary To provide administrative office support services to the chair, faculty and students of the Department of Modern Languages and Cultures. This position is full time September through June and part time (20 hours per week) during July and August. Position Description: Modern Languages and Cultures Department Support(90%) Serve as front-line support and knowledgeable resource for all students, faculty, and guests of the Modern Languages and Cultures Department, maintaining an open-door policy and welcoming environment. Answer incoming calls, e-mail messages and questions from students and general public. Provide multi-faceted programmatic and operational support, including classroom assignments, office assignments, key requisitions and making copies of essential department projects as requested. Support departmental meetings, send reminders, and record, write, and distribute minutes. Track that tasks assigned during meetings are accomplished. Developing and/or updating department promotional materials and keeping copies available at all times. Troubleshooting faculty and student requests. Maintain confidential student records, process student Registrar forms. Maintain department statistical data including annual lists of active minors and majors and keep current student email lists and academic advising handouts. Use student and learning management systems and internal data delivery applications needed for registering students, auditing degree requirements, and generating admissions and enrollment reports. Manage Challenge Exams schedules and results during the four quarters of the year, to be sent to the Office of the Registrar as well as communicated to students. Maintain accuracy and relevance of department website. Update regularly with student\u2019s spotlight section, changes in any language and events. Coordinate logistics for department events including creating flyers, securing room reservations, catering orders, and guest parking plus assisting with advertising and overseeing day-of-event activities. Hire, train, and supervise a part-time student office assistant. Assist with the hiring and interviews of new faculty. Manage on-boarding of part-time faculty including requesting and tracking Letters of Appointment, and updating schedules with the names of new instructors. Assist faculty with departmental computer refresh needs. Prepare Modern Languages and Cultures Department Program Review documents and assist in any tasks related to completing the process. Assist with annual academic catalog updates and support ongoing program assessment. Scheduling Duties \u00a0 Coordinate with department chair to assemble and maintain quarterly schedule of classes. Enter and track quarterly course schedule, make periodic reviews and changes as needed, and monitor the schedules to make sure that the changes are reflected on the system prior to the opening registration date. Prepare and submit curriculum forms. Update faculty teaching assignments. Work to resolve scheduling conflicts and room requests. Monitor wait lists and manually register students when needed. Track enrollment caps during student registration periods. Maintain booking calendar in Outlook for shared department conference room. Financial Activity Management Prepare requests for all department and faculty led study abroad programs expenditures and reimbursement requests including assigning correct activity and accounting codes and monitoring purchases to assure they are within budget limitations and in accordance with University polices. Assist with faculty reimbursements through ProcureSU. Manage Procard account for department. Process honorarium requests. Manage and purchase office supplies. Other duties as assigned. College Committee Support or Other Approved Service (10%) Provide administrative and logistical support for College committee activities, including: Support for Faculty-Staff Senate (or similar) Organize and attend virtual meetings (approximately 9 standing meetings). Prepare and post meeting minutes. Provide logistical and event support including catering coordination (approximately 4 events annually). College Committee Elections Coordinate the annual College committee election process in collaboration with FSS senators and the Dean\u2019s Office. Identify vacant committee positions; prepare election communications; solicit and track nominations; develop and administer Qualtrics ballots; and communicate election results. Qualifications High school diploma and three or more years of previous administrative support experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Demonstrated ability to form and maintain productive working relationships with faculty, staff, and students from diverse backgrounds. Customer service driven with self-directed problem-solving skills. Strong and professional written and oral communication skills to be able to proof and correct spelling, grammar, and punctuation in any department promotional materials or communications. Proven ability to work in a busy office environment, with frequent interruptions and competing deadlines, while maintaining a high degree of accuracy, attention to detail, and a warm and welcoming environment. Proven ability to handle confidential information with a high degree of tact and sound judgment. Experience handling the logistics of setting up meetings and other events, which may include guest parking, advertising, and catering plus overseeing day-of-event activities. Intermediate level PC-based computer skills, with strong proficiency using Microsoft Windows and Office software including Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint, and Word. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree. Proficiency in another language. Experience working in a previous administrative support position in a university setting, with familiarity using student and learning management systems. Compensation at a Glance $22.80 per hour - $23.86 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Professor--Biology_JR100535": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Biology", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nPosition Description The Biology Department at Seattle University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position with expertise in neurobiology, beginning in August 2027. This appointment supports the launch of an interdisciplinary undergraduate Neuroscience major spanning multiple departments and colleges across the University. \u00a0The successful candidate will be responsible for teaching both introductory and advanced courses within our undergraduate biology curriculum; developing new courses for the Neuroscience program; providing service to the program, department, college and university; and establishing an active, extramurally funded research program that engages undergraduates in neurobiology (e.g., cellular/molecular neuroscience, systems or circuit neuroscience, developmental neurobiology, behavioral neuroscience, computational neuroscience, or comparative approaches).\u00a0 Minimum qualifications include a Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Biology, or a related field; post-doctoral training; and undergraduate teaching experience. For this position, we offer a competitive start-up package, a well-equipped research facility, faculty mentoring, and a collaborative campus community located in the vibrant city of Seattle.\u00a0 Applicants should submit applications online at www.seattleu.edu/careers including: Cover letter\u00a0summarizing your qualifications and potential for contributions to Seattle University. In your cover letter, explain how you understand, embody, and would contribute to each of the following at Seattle University. Please dedicate one paragraph to each prompt: Our 5-year Strategic Plan, Reigniting Our Strategic Directions; Our Mission as a Jesuit and Catholic University; Our commitment to DEI and Inclusive Academic Excellence; Our focus on pedagogy, scholarship, service, and institution building; Our faculty members\u2019 role as Teacher-Scholars. Curriculum vitae\u00a0(CV) Research statement (describing current and planned research program)\u00a0 Teaching statement (including philosophy, proposed course(s), and experience with course/curriculum development)\u00a0 Unofficial copies of undergraduate and graduate transcripts\u00a0 Names and contact information for three references\u00a0 Letters of reference may be solicited upon completion of\u00a0application. Review of\u00a0applications\u00a0will begin November 1, 2026.\u00a0 The position will remain open until filled and is contingent on availability of funding.\u00a0Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Carolyn Stenbak (stenbakc@seattleu.edu).\u00a0 Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: $75,000-$85,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Professor--Electrical-and-Computer-Engineering_JR100514": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Electrical and Computer Engineering", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nPosition Description The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Seattle University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning in August 2027. We seek candidates with expertise in computer engineering, particularly areas such as VLSI, robotics hardware and control, and machine learning, as well as a strong interest in innovative and effective teaching practices. \u00a0 The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring students; an established record of research with enthusiasm for involving undergraduate students; an interest in pursuing external funding; and a genuine desire to contribute to the mission of Seattle University. Responsibilities will include teaching, including laboratory courses; maintaining an active research program that provides meaningful opportunities for undergraduate participation; and contributing service to the university. Requirements: Ph.D. in electrical or computer engineering or closely related field. The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department is housed within the College of Science and Engineering. The undergraduate electrical engineering and computer engineering programs are ABET-accredited and support the University\u2019s mission of educating the whole person, professional formation, and empowering leaders for a just and humane world. For more information about the Electrical and Computer Engineering Department at Seattle University, visit our website at http://www.seattleu.edu/scieng/ece/ . Applicants should submit applications online at www.seattleu.edu/careers including: Cover letter\u00a0summarizing your qualifications and potential for contributions to Seattle University. In your cover letter, explain how you understand, embody, and would contribute to each of the following at Seattle University. Please dedicate one paragraph to each prompt: Our 5-year Strategic Plan, Reigniting Our Strategic Directions;\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Our Mission as a Jesuit and Catholic University; Our commitment to DEI and Inclusive Academic Excellence; Our focus on pedagogy, scholarship, service, and institution building; Our faculty members\u2019 role as Teacher-Scholars. Curriculum vitae\u00a0(CV) Research statement (describing current and planned research program)\u00a0 Teaching statement (including philosophy, proposed course(s), and experience with course/curriculum development)\u00a0 Names and contact information for three references\u00a0 Letters of reference may be solicited upon completion of\u00a0application. For full consideration, applications should be submitted by November 1, 2026.\u00a0The position will remain open until filled and is contingent on availability of funding.\u00a0 Inquiries regarding the position should be directed to Dr. Shiny Abraham at abrahash@seattleu.edu . Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: $92,000-$102,000 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Project-Center-Director_JR100515": {"title": "Project Center Director", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Seattle University has an exciting, dynamic opportunity for a Project Center Director to join our community. The Project Center Director provides leadership and oversight for the College of Science and Engineering Project Center. The Director manages Project Center staff and operations and leads efforts in project recruitment, industry and community outreach, marketing, contract management, event planning, and program administration. The Director works closely with faculty to support the development and delivery of sponsored student projects. The Director also cultivates and maintains external relationships with companies, government agencies, nonprofit organizations, alumni, and other external partners to recruit project sponsors and expand opportunities for students to engage in meaningful, real-world projects. Major responsibilities include project recruitment, contract management, marketing, events planning, and project management, contract management. Internal engagement with college leaderships, faculty, support staff and students is also required. Position Description: Essential Job Functions Project Recruitment/Business Development: Research potential leads; participate in business development networking activities; create lead lists; develop relationships; develop project concepts collaboratively with sponsor liaisons and SU faculty. Work with college leadership, and the development officer to identify strategic external relationship goals for projects and beyond. \u00a0 Contract Management and Sponsor Relations: Work collaboratively with sponsor liaisons and SU faculty to identify and refine project scopes. Hands-on management of all contractual materials affiliated with projects to include intellectual property agreements, sponsor fees, master agreements, and deliverables. \u00a0 Marketing Management: Work creatively to maximize marketing opportunities for program involvement. Appeal to internal audience of students and faculty for program functions, administration and morale. Also engage alumni and potential sponsors to generate interest, attendance, and involvement in program events and ultimately projects. Coordinate all marketing materials for Project Center, including photography and copy for brochures, Projects Day program, website, and social media outlets. \u00a0 Events Planning: Supervise and execute fall workshops, joint sessions, and the annual Projects Day program. Projects Day planning includes administrative support to students and guidance regarding final deliverables, printed materials, final presentations, and Projects Day conference. Oversee administrative staff in events planning support of these functions. \u00a0 Program Management: Work closely with department faculty to identify key areas of interest and needs for Project Center. Create and administer all program feedback and surveys for faculty, students, and sponsors. Work with college leadership, students, and department coordinators to address issues and facilitate interactions with the sponsors. Oversee administrative staff on execution of administrative duties to include student relations and budgets affiliated with projects. \u00a0 Perform other related duties as assigned. \u00a0 Essential Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree and five years of successful technical sales or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job such as those listed above. \u00a0 Experience in oral and written presentations with corporate and governmental managers and groups. \u00a0 Background in program development including marketing, sales, event planning, and fundraising. \u00a0 Strong ability to develop corporate relationships and work collaboratively within an academic setting. \u00a0 All candidates must show a demonstrated commitment to diversity and the university\u2019s mission, vision, and values. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Strong local industry and government contacts. Involvement with local technology groups and societies. \u00a0 Application Instructions Applicants should attach an electronic cover letter and resume when applying. Compensation at a Glance $91,500 \u2013 $98,500 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Maintenance-Plumber_JR100364": {"title": "Lead Maintenance Plumber", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Reporting to the Director of Operations and Maintenance, and under the priorities established by the shop lead, this position provides plumbing and pipefitting services as an integral part of the operations and maintenance of the university. Position Description: Operations and Maintenance (85%) Perform technical duties in the general maintenance of all mechanical systems including installing, repairing or replacing plumbing, heating, ventilation, and air conditioning equipment. Analyze the effectiveness of current materials and systems and recommending changes to improve the cost effectiveness or energy efficiency of existing systems. Inspect, diagnose malfunctions, and repair, pressure regulators, tanks, piping and auxiliary devices on RO/DI systems, swimming pools, water features, potable water, air, vacuum, gas, and drainage systems. Install, service, repair and adjust pumps, valves, stills, heat exchangers, and coils. Assist in the repair of heating, cooling, and ventilation systems. Repair, replace or install all types of plumbing fixtures such as water closets, sinks, showers, drinking fountains, kitchen, laboratory, and other equipment. Auger drain lines. Diagnose and correct potable water cross connections, water pressure issues, and retention systems. Construct various types of pipe joints for high or low-pressure systems, including threading, brazing, soldering. Cut, fit, fabricate, and assemble steel, copper, brass, glass, plastic, cast iron pipe. Test and maintain backflow assembly devices. Design and build piping assemblies using standard piping practices. Assist in identifying and implementing energy and water conservation projects. Provide direction and inspect contractor's work to see that work is performed to campus specifications. Recommend purchase of equipment and supplies for the shop operation, maintenance and repairs. Read and interpret plans, blueprints, and sketches. Order materials and supplies as required. Perform other duties of a similar nature or level. Plan and organize assigned work to fulfill task standards, meet schedule requirements, and maintain workload balance. Work autonomously or as part of a team, depending on the project or problem being addressed. Properly and safely use appropriate tools used in performing required tasks. Account for and maintain university-owned tools and equipment; repair and maintain tools as needed. Maintain a safe and clean shop space. Follow and promote established safety rules and regulations; attend safety trainings as required. Lockout tagout procedures must be followed. Work in a safe, clean and respectful manner while performing work on campus. Work in occupied spaces with minimal disruption of campus activities. Scheduling (5%) Schedule work taking into account customer requests, space available and potential disruption to adjacent academic, work and living areas. Communication and Customer service (5%) Communicate with the Shop Lead, the requester, and other staff as needed regarding development and status of work requests. Coordinate with the Facilities Managers on project request recommendations, estimates, and work management reports as required. Administrative (5%) Open, update, track and complete all work orders assigned. Complete all paperwork and tracking required for personal time sheets in a timely manner. Attend departmental meetings as required. Marginal Functions Supervise student workers Minimum Qualifications WA State Journeyman\u2019s Plumbers License; Required to obtain City of Seattle 3rd Grade Steam Engineers License, AS-1 Sprinkler Confidence Testing License, BOC Certificate, Backflow Certification, City of Seattle Gas piping License. Preferred Qualifications City of Seattle Refrigeration Journeyman/Operator License and Washington State HVAC/refrigeration system (6B) License. Compensation at a Glance Hourly Rate: $47.82 Annual Salary: approximately $99,465.60 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Academic-Advisor--Undergraduate_JR100501": {"title": "Academic Advisor, Undergraduate", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Undergraduate Academic Advisors guide students through educational planning and registration processes; assist students in resolving challenges to educational progress; and connect students to information, opportunities, and resources at the University. Academic Advisors advise a caseload of students assigned by academic program, interacting with students individually in scheduled and drop-in appointments, in small groups, online, and through occasional events and programs. Undergraduate Academic Advisors collaborate with campus partners, faculty, and academic departments to ensure timely and accurate information in support of student success. This position works as part of an advising center team co-located in a specific school or college and reporting to the University Advising office. Academic Advisors educate the whole student through holistic and proactive advising services. Position Description: Academic Advising 70% of role Engages students through intentional processes of educational and career planning, based on thoughtful discernment of goals and carried out through regular review and revision. The processes may include exploring/declaring/changing/adding majors, minors, and/or career paths and developing course and degree plans that incorporate co-curricular activities. Helps students understand degree requirements, assists them with appropriate course selection and planning during quarterly registration periods; guides students to reflect on their progress and reviews final degree requirements. Attends to the various planning needs of individual students including but not limited to international students, student athletes, military-connected students, First Generation students, and prospective students who are considering enrollment at Seattle University.\u00a0 Assists students in their academic and personal adjustment to Seattle University and with issues that could affect their educational progress.\u00a0 This assistance may include outreach to students who are struggling academically, responding to early warning processes and referrals to students in need of support, and arranging academic and personal interventions as appropriate.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Guides students through problem-solving on a wide variety of concerns, ranging from registration to personal and life issues, and refers them to resources for academic development and personal support. Informs students about available resources and options for university engagement; collaborates with relevant offices and promotes participation in such co-curricular activities as studying abroad, community engagement, leadership development, research, and internships.\u00a0 Provides timely and accurate responses to student inquiries via email or text and utilizing CRM (customer relationship management) software. Maintains accurate records of advising conversations and tracks student progress through data collection and analysis in CRM software. Collaboration with Campus Partners 20% of role Coordinates with assigned academic departments to ensure access to up-to-date information, including degree requirements, recommended pathways, and course scheduling. Serves as a resource to faculty and campus partners regarding academic policy and procedures. Participates in campus events for prospective students and families, as well as student life-cycle events such as convocation and commencement. Develops and produces written (print and online) resources as requested. May serve on campus-wide committees to represent academic advising and work on academic initiatives.\u00a0 Participates in university-wide academic advising efforts and initiatives, including data collection. Professional and Team Development 10% of role Participates in internal and external professional development and training opportunities. Participates in team meetings and activities. Supports the Lead Advisor and team in coverage of the advising center office and availability for drop-in hours. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree and two years previous experience working in an educational environment or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job. All candidates must demonstrate\u00a0experience and ability to work successfully with diverse populations and\u00a0a desire\u00a0to contribute to a team that\u00a0is\u00a0dedicated to anti-racist principles and promotes equity. Effective communication and interpersonal skills with excellent listening, writing, and speaking skills. Experience working with students who are academically or personally struggling. Prepared to work in a fast-paced environment that embraces change and variety. Effective decision making and problem-solving skills. Proficiency in MS Office Software such as Excel, Word, Outlook, PowerPoint, and Teams. All positions at Seattle University require a criminal history background check. Preferred qualifications Master\u2019s degree and/or three years of experience in higher education. Experience interpreting, communicating, and applying academic policies. Experience with CRM software, particularly Slate, and other online tools. Knowledge of program development, program evaluation, and student development. Previous experience and skills in the use of student data management systems. An understanding of and appreciation for student success in a Jesuit institution of higher education. Compensation at a Glance $55,000 - $56,500 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Director-of-Counseling-Field-Experience-and-Compliance_JR100490": {"title": "Director of Counseling Field Experience and Compliance", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Director of Counseling Field Experience and Compliance provides strategic leadership and oversight of students\u2019 clinical training experiences in alignment with standards established by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP). This role focuses on ensuring that practicum and internship experiences are developmentally appropriate, rigorously supervised, and structured to support the attainment of core counseling competencies and professional identity. This position is responsible for the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of practicum and internship experiences that meet CACREP requirements across all programs in the Counseling Department. This includes ensuring that students engage in required direct and indirect service hours, receive qualified individual and group supervision, and participate in ongoing evaluation processes that assess clinical skills, ethical practice, and professional dispositions. The Director works closely with faculty and site supervisors to align clinical experiences with curricular goals, support student development, and intervene with faculty to provide additional guidance or remediation when needed to ensure timely graduate degree completion. Emphasis is placed on the quality, consistency, and educational integrity of the student experience across practicum and internship. The Director establishes standards for supervision, develops training and orientation resources for faculty and site supervisors, monitors student progress, and ensures that all clinical experiences adhere to ethical guidelines of the American Counseling Association (ACA) and accreditation expectations of CACREP. In addition, the position leads efforts to maintain accurate and transparent professional licensure disclosures in accordance with federal regulations and CACREP-aligned program standards. This includes monitoring state licensure requirements for multiple states, communicating how the program curriculum supports licensure eligibility, and advising students on post-graduation pathways toward credentialing. The Director is responsible for portions of the accreditation processes by overseeing documentation, assessment data, and reporting related to practicum and internship outcomes. This position works closely with the CACREP liaison to ensure compliance with CACREP field experience requirements. The role requires professional expertise in counselor education, clinical supervision, and licensure processes, as well as strong leadership, organizational, and communication skills to ensure high-quality clinical training experiences and successful student progression toward professional practice. Position Description: Professional Practice in Clinical Training (PPCT) (70%) Responsible for the clinical training process, ensuring effective operation of clinical practicums and internships by organizing and evaluating clinical sites, analyzing clinical site data, and supporting students, site supervisors, and faculty supervisors. Collaborate and meet quarterly with practicum and internship instructors to ensure there is synchronicity across field experiences as it relates to state and CACREP clinical field experience requirements. Develop, maintain, and implement the goals, policies, standards, and procedures for the departmental PPCT manuals for students and site supervisors. Address concerns related to practicum and internship students' academic, skills, and professional dispositions in collaboration with Program Directors, Student Development Chair, and appropriate faculty advisor. Develop and deliver practicum and internship orientation for site supervisors to orient them to the CACREP supervision and field experience requirements. Monitor policies and procedures used in the field experience courses to ensure they meet CACREP requirements. Maintain collaborative partnerships with diverse community agencies and stakeholders that align with the Counseling Department and College of Education\u2019s social justice mission. Ensure that counseling students have opportunities to work with diverse clients in professional practice. Maintain regular contact with practicum and internship counseling faculty, constituents, and supervisors at clinical sites to strengthen relationships with partner institutions. Ensure that affiliation agreements are completed between the university and placement sites. Ensure support for prospective and current site supervisors and community partners through the vetting and placement processes, and clinical training experience. Responsible for the regular clinical field experience assessments of students and site supervisors during each academic term and analyzing satisfaction surveys from students and site supervisors at the end of professional practice. Ensure the effectiveness of clinical evaluation tools and methods to determine consistency with learning outcomes and objectives. Collaborate with field experience faculty and site supervisors to address student clinical issues as they arise. Other duties as assigned. \u00a0 CACREP Accreditation (15%) Oversee documentation, assessment data, and reporting related to practicum and internship outcomes. Support the CACREP Liaison as a contact for students, faculty, supervisors, licensing boards, and accrediting bodies to support continuous program improvement and ensure compliance with CACREP standards around the field experience. Collaborate with the CACREP Liaison to ensure all counseling programs maintain compliance with CACREP standards through regular internal program evaluation including annual review of syllabi. Provide the CACREP Liaison with support on the organization and development of all reaccreditation requirements including any required self-study, site visit preparation and reporting, etc. Other duties as assigned. \u00a0 Professional Licensure Disclosure (15%) Annually review multiple state statutes and administrative codes for educational requirements for licensure. Collaborate with counseling program leaders to update students and faculty on certification and licensure mandates. Provide incoming and continuing counseling students with information sessions for obtaining licensing and credentials at significant benchmarks throughout their program. Advise students and faculty on PPCT requirements, licensing exams, external workshops, and courses that relate to internship and licensing (i.e., ACA, WMHCA). Other duties as assigned. \u00a0 Required Qualifications \u00a0 Doctoral degree in a counselor education program from a CACREP-accredited program. Experience as an instructor of record in master\u2019s and/or doctorate-level clinical mental health or school counseling instruction in a post-secondary or college institution. Familiarity with CACREP regulations pertaining to field placement and field experience. Familiarity with counselor licensure/certification policy and regulations, or state administrative statutes and codes. Experience within an education environment (K-12 or higher education) and/or mental health agency. Experience with Microsoft 365, including strong proficiency in Excel, SharePoint, Teams, and Outlook. Experience with data management systems. Excellent organizational, attention to detail, and interpersonal communication skills, both verbal and written. Demonstrated success in a fast-paced environment, working collaboratively, and communicating effectively to promote teamwork, collegiality, and wellness. Proven ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with discretion and professionalism. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications \u00a0 A minimum of 2-3 years of experience supervising clinical or school counseling students and faculty. Membership in a national professional counseling association. Current full state licensing/certification (e.g., LPC, LMHC, ESA, PSC). Certification as an NCC or similar NBCC credentialed counselor. Demonstrated commitment to social justice. Experience with diverse student populations. Familiarity with SimCare, ELC (Tevera), Canvas, Qualtrics, and related software. Flexibility and willingness to assume new tasks and special projects. Compensation at a Glance $39.95 per hour - $46.15 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Counseling-Placement-Program-Manager_JR100472": {"title": "Counseling Placement Program Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Counseling Placement Program Manager serves two primary functions for the Counseling Department within the School of Education (SOE): 1.) placement coordination for practicums and internships and 2.) management of professional licensure disclosure regulations for the On-Ground Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC), On-Ground School Counseling (SC), Online CMHC, and Online SC programs. This position will identify, approve, and secure counseling practicum and internship sites that meet state and CACREP accreditation requirements. This work also supports diversity, equity, and inclusion in the counseling profession by ensuring that practicum and internship placements provide students with an opportunity to work with marginalized client populations. The Counseling Placement Program Manager will also oversee a placement orientation for counseling students, provide professional-level support to secure field placements, track practicum and internship data and paperwork, establish community partnerships, and oversee the professional licensure disclosure data for the counseling programs. Position Description: Practicum and Internship Placement Coordination (75%) \u00a0 Identify, develop, and maintain collaborative partnerships with diverse community agencies and stakeholders that align with the Counseling Department and SOE\u2019s social justice mission. Ensure practicum and internship placements meet state and national accreditation standards. Support partner initiatives, collaborations, and outreach efforts to identify future placements. Build and maintain a database in SimCare for all clinical mental health-based and K-12 partnerships for the Counseling Department. Create, maintain, and modify all SimCare forms for placements. Collect, analyze, and report on practicum and internship progress to support accreditation efforts. Facilitate practicum and internship placement information sessions for counseling students. Collaborate with PPCT Director to update and maintain field experience handbooks for the counseling programs. Provide practicum and internship search preparation and support to students. Collaborate with data administrator, program directors, and administrative support to develop and maintain placement Canvas course.\u00a0 Maintain complete and accurate program records to ensure data collection and analysis. Collaborate with PPCT Director to create and provide training for site supervisors including, but not limited to, expectations, program specifics, and supervision. Administer site supervisor and student satisfaction surveys at the end of the field experience. Other duties as assigned. \u00a0 Professional Licensure Disclosure Coordination (25%) \u00a0 Build and maintain a program of study requirements for out-of-state counseling students. Collaborate with counseling program directors to update students and faculty on certification and licensure mandates. Provide counseling students with information sessions for obtaining licensing and credentials at significant benchmarks throughout their program. Advise students and faculty on PPCT requirements, licensing exams, external workshops and courses that relate to internship and licensing (i.e., ACA, WMHCA). Maintain professional licensure disclosure forms for students. Other duties as assigned. \u00a0 Required Qualifications \u00a0 Master\u2019s degree in counseling from a CACREP program or other closely related disciplines from an accredited institution plus 2 or more years of experience with placement coordination for practicums and internships OR an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job. Familiarity with CACREP regulations. Familiarity with counselor licensure/certification policy and regulations, or state administrative statutes and codes. Experience within an education environment (K-12 or higher education) and/or mental health agency. Experience with Microsoft 365, including strong proficiency in Excel, SharePoint, Teams, and Outlook. Experience with data management systems. Excellent organizational, attention to detail, and interpersonal communication skills, both verbal and written. Demonstrated success in a fast-paced environment, working collaboratively, and communicating effectively to promote teamwork, collegiality, and wellness. Proven ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with discretion and professionalism. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple tasks with competing deadlines. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Licensed as a mental health counselor. Demonstrated commitment to social justice. Experience with diverse student populations. Experience with SimCare, ELC, Canvas, and Qualtrics. Flexibility and willingness to assume new tasks and special projects. Compensation at a Glance $33.33 per hour - $38.46 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Temporary-Admissions-Application-Readers_JR100564": {"title": "Temporary Admissions Application Readers", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nMonths Per Year: 5 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Seattle University is seeking detail-oriented temporary undergraduate admissions application readers to help meet our undergraduate application review goals. Position Description: Reporting to the Senior Associate Director of Recruitment, the ideal candidate will have experience in undergraduate admissions or college counseling, and will have familiarity with the Common Application and holistic application review. Qualifications : Bachelor\u2019s degree At-home computer or laptop with wi-fi access 2 or more years of experience in Undergraduate Admissions or College Counseling Strong attention to detail Ability to work 10-20 hours per week Located in Washington state Ability to come to Seattle University campus for onboarding and training Familiarity with the Slate CRM is preferred \u00a0 This is a temporary position, beginning in late September/early October and will end in late February/early March. Compensation at a Glance $21.30-$25.00 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/One-Stop-Services-Counselor_JR100516": {"title": "One Stop Services Counselor", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Reporting to the Assistant Director of Student Contact and Communication, this position is the primary contact for students, prospective students, and family members regarding both Financial Aid and Student Account related questions. This position provides customer services and counseling for both financial aid and student account related activities and contact may take place over phone, in-person, via email or virtual video appointments.\u00a0 Position Description: Essential Functions and Associated Tasks Financial Aid (45%): Assist students and families with questions regarding applying for financial aid.\u00a0 Explain the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA), including eligibility and filing issues, and assist with its completion as necessary.\u00a0 Answer questions about financial aid packages and the timing of awards.\u00a0 Provide information on types of loans (federal, state, and private) and assist students with the complex process associated with loans.\u00a0 Explain the financial aid verification process and assist students and families with documents required to satisfy verification.\u00a0\u00a0 Explain satisfactory academic progress, requirements, and the eligibility appeal process. Assist students with Financial Aid special circumstance appeals and how financial aid decisions are made.\u00a0 Explain student\u2019s rights for financial aid and the student\u2019s responsibility in the Financial Aid process. Explain all financial aid questions that can be answered in a 5\u20137-minute timeframe that are not confidential in nature.\u00a0 Provide information about scholarships and grants for all student body populations, i.e., undergraduate, and graduate students and how to maintain eligibility. Provide information on federal and state grant qualifications. \u00a0 Student Account Management (45%): Provide information and assist with payment and financing options, payment plans, refunds, billing schedule, account balances, lost checks, waiver requests (e.g., health insurance and late fees), 1098-T, paid receipts and holds.\u00a0\u00a0Provide counseling to students, prospective students, and family members about educational finance options. Online Services (10%): Assist students, prospective students and families with various online services including: MySeattleU, FAFSA and the studentaid.gov website for federal loan information. Qualifications Bachelor's degree plus a minimum of two years of experience working in a Financial Aid/Student Account office, or an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential duties of the job. Demonstrated commitment to strong customer services and the ability to resolve wide-ranging complex problems through creative reasoning and logic to accurately determine the cause and the resolution to the problems in an effective, innovative and timely manner. Applicant must possess the skills to counsel individuals and families in financial aid and student accounts matters and work both independently and cooperatively with other staff members and other departments. Desktop PC, ERP system and Microsoft Office Suite experience are required. Demonstrated ability to interpret and present information and ideas clearly and accurately in writing, orally and by preparation of reports and other materials.\u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Two or more years working in a Financial Aid/Student Accounts Office. Prior experience using the Ellucian Colleague ERP. Biligual (Spanish preferred). Compensation at a Glance $46,000 - $47,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Project-Coordinator--A-OCAY_JR100600": {"title": "Project Coordinator, A-OCAY", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary We are seeking a highly organized and detail-oriented individual to join our team as a Project Coordinator for the Achieving Optimal Childhood, Adolescence and Young Adulthood (A-OCAY): An integrated, holistic, and community-engaged model for interprofessional behavioral health workforce education and training in the Evergreen State grant. The successful candidate will be responsible for coordinating grant project activities, collecting trainee data, liaising with university and community partners, monitoring grant deliverables, contributing to required progress reports, and assisting to establish and manage the new continuing education program. This role requires excellent communication and organizational skills, project management, and basic financial skills, as well as the ability to work collaboratively with multiple stakeholders. This position is under the supervision of the Project Director, Dr. Tam Dinh. This is a grant-funded, benefit-eligible position with an end date of 6/30/2029. Position Description: Key Areas of Responsibility \u00a0 Coordinate project activities: Plan and organize project timelines, milestones, and deliverables. Collaborate with team members to ensure smooth execution of project plans.\u00a0 Collect trainee data: Work with the Project Director to design and implement data collection processes to gather information from trainees participating in the project. Maintain accurate and up-to-date records of trainee data.\u00a0 Liaise with university and community partners: Establish and maintain strong working relationships with project team (MSW, MACFT, DNP faculty and staff) and community partners.\u00a0 Coordinate communication, collaboration, and information exchange between project team and community partners. Collaborate with MSW, MACFT, and DNP field coordinators to ensure trainees are placed in training sites\u00a0 Work with trainees to ensure grant requirements are met (commitment letter, NPI, outcome data collection)\u00a0 Monitor grant deliverables: Track project progress and ensure compliance with grant requirements. Monitor timelines, milestones, and deliverables outlined in the grant proposal. Work with the Project Director to prepare and submit progress reports to funding agencies.\u00a0 Write reports: Compile and analyze project data to create comprehensive reports for internal and external stakeholders. Present findings, outcomes, and recommendations in a clear and concise manner. Work with the Project Director and Office of Sponsored Research to prepare financial reports related to project expenses and budget utilization.\u00a0 Manage the continuing education process: Develop and implement strategies to facilitate continuing education opportunities for project participants. Coordinate workshops, seminars, and other educational activities. Evaluate the effectiveness of the continuing education program and make recommendations for improvement.\u00a0 Manage trainee, clinical supervisor/preceptor/community advisory board payments: Coordinate trainee payment processes, ensuring accuracy and timeliness. Work closely with the Project Director and Office of Sponsored Research to process stipends, reimbursements, or other financial arrangements in compliance with project and grant requirements.\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications \u00a0 Bachelor's degree in a relevant field (e.g., project management, healthcare administration, social sciences).\u00a0 Proven experience in project coordination or a similar role\u00a0 Strong organizational and time management skills, with the ability to prioritize tasks and meet deadlines.\u00a0 Excellent communication skills, both written and verbal. Ability to effectively communicate with diverse stakeholders.\u00a0 Proficiency in data collection, analysis, and reporting.\u00a0 Knowledge of grant management processes and familiarity with grant compliance requirements.\u00a0 Experience in managing budgets and financial aspects of projects.\u00a0 Experience in coordinating and managing continuing education programs is a plus.\u00a0 Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment.\u00a0 Proficiency in project management software and tools.\u00a0 All candidates must demonstrate commitment to diversity and the university's mission, vision, and values. Compensation at a Glance $65,776 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Benefits are available to eligible employees working at an FTE of 0.53 or greater. Eligible employees may choose from three medical plans, as well as dental and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short- and long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution toward retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions, up to a maximum of 5%. Eligible employees may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for themselves and eligible dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an on-site fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid December holiday break closure, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/International-Transfer-Admissions-Counselor_JR100641": {"title": "International Transfer Admissions Counselor", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The International Transfer Admissions Counselor will join a dynamic undergraduate admissions team that represents Seattle University's Jesuit academic and co-curricular opportunities to prospective transfer students, their families and supporters, and the transfer advising community. This position works primarily with transfer students with an additional strategic focus on international transfer students. In addition, the counseling team supports all prospective students, including First-Year, Transfer, Arts, Pre-College, and Post-Baccalaureate, by providing advising and support through email and phone communication, appointments, events on and off campus, and other outreach programs as needed. Position Description: 1. Recruitment Management - 40% Provide information about the University's academic opportunities to prospective students, families, counselors, advisors, and other stakeholders through presentations, emails, calls, and one on one conversation. Plan, develop, and implement on and off campus recruitment travel and activities. Conduct regular outreach to international centers at community colleges and plan recruitment events specifically for international students. Meet with prospective students, including adult learners and traditional aged college students, through various channels, including community college visits, community college fairs campus appointments, information sessions, and workshops. Collaborate with campus stakeholders to meet undergraduate enrollment goals. Engage in efforts to make higher education more accessible, equitable, and inclusive; participate in working groups to embed these efforts in job responsibilities. Approximately 10 to 12 weeks of in state and out of state travel. Most travel is in the greater Puget Sound Region. 2. Application Review and Recommendations - 25% Review, evaluate, and provide initial undergraduate admission recommendations based on university criteria and artistic standards (where applicable) 3. International Transfer Admissions - 15% Monitor submission of international students\u2019 submitted materials for I-20 issuance and provides regular outreach to students and updates to leadership team members. Serve as a Designated School Official (DSO), which involves issuing initial I-20's for incoming international students. Work in close partnership with the International Student and Scholar Center and participates in regular DSO trainings. Communicate with students on missing materials needed for I-20 issuance. Serves as a member of the undergraduate international admissions group which includes the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment and the Admissions Evaluator-International Credential Evaluations. Participate in regular meetings with the group and supports international functions in close collaboration with the Sr. Assistant Director for International Recruitment. Assist with other duties related to international admissions as needed. \u00a0 4. Event Attendance - 10% Support and actively participate in programs and events engaging prospective First Year, Transfer, Post Baccalaureate, and Pre College students, their families, community organizations, college counselors, and advisors. Attend major admission events such as preview days and admitted student days. Evening and weekend work required, especially in fall and spring. 4. Other Duties - 10% Manage and work on assigned project areas throughout the year. Other duties as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Education Requirements Minimum: Bachelor's Degree (4-year) in a relevant field. Experience Minimum of 1 year of professional work experience, or a combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the duties and responsibilities of the job. Minimum Qualifications Must possess well-developed organizational skills and attention to detail. Confidence in public speaking to both large and small groups. Strong oral and written communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain cooperative and effective working relationships with others. A commitment to diversity, equity and inclusion, and supporting diverse student populations. Experience with or connection to international education or work. Ability and willingness to travel and work evenings and weekends as necessary. Valid driver's license required. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Speaks, writes, and reads one of the following languages: Spanish, Vietnamese, Somali, Mandarin, Tagalog, Amharic, or Tigrinya, and is interested in utilizing language skills to serve prospective students and families in the college search process. Knowledge of Microsoft Office products and federal and state privacy laws. Experience using an ERP and CRM, specifically Workday and Technolutions Slate CRM. Experience working with diverse young adult and youth populations. Experience working with international students (international student center, immigration support, study abroad, international admissions, etc.) . \u00a0 Physical Demands This position performs work in an office environment. Frequency values: Never; Seldom (1\u201310%, 0\u20131 hour); Occasional (11\u201333%, 1\u20133 hours); Frequent (34\u201366%, 3\u20136 hours); Constant (67\u2013100%, not restricted). \u00a0 Travel - Frequent Disclaimer This job description is intended to provide an overview of the purpose of this role and its duties and responsibilities. The description is not intended to be a comprehensive list of the work to be performed. Additional tasks may be assigned from time to time, or the scope of the job may be altered based on need. Nothing included in this description alters the at-will employment relationship between the employee and the University. Compensation at a Glance $52,000 - $53,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Benefits are available to eligible employees working at an FTE of 0.53 or greater. Eligible employees may choose from three medical plans, as well as dental and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short- and long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution toward retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions, up to a maximum of 5%. Eligible employees may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for themselves and eligible dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an on-site fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid December holiday break closure, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Senior-Assistant-Director-of-Admissions--Transfer-Recruitment_JR100631": {"title": "Senior Assistant Director of Admissions, Transfer Recruitment", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Hybrid Work Eligible Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary Under the direction of the Senior Associate Director of Operations and Transfer Admission, the Senior Assistant Director of Admissions, Transfer Recruitment leads Seattle University\u2019s transfer student recruitment efforts while supervising and mentoring the Transfer Recruitment Team. The role serves as a primary contact for transfer students and community college partners, manages a recruitment territory, oversees transfer application review and admission recommendations, and helps achieve undergraduate enrollment goals. The position also provides leadership for admissions counseling staff, develops training programs, supports student advising and outreach, and represents the university at recruitment and admission events. Strong leadership, relationship-building, problem-solving, and student-centered communication skills are essential, along with flexibility for frequent evening and weekend work during peak recruitment seasons. Position Description: Admissions Counseling & Application Review \u2013 40% Advise prospective students throughout the admissions process through appointments, phone calls, events, and other outreach. Review and evaluate transfer applications. Make initial admission recommendations in alignment with university policies and selection criteria. Support counseling efforts for all undergraduate student populations as needed. Team Leadership & Staff Development \u2013 30% Supervise and provide leadership for the Transfer Recruitment Team. Mentor Admissions Counseling staff and foster a collaborative, student-centered culture. Develop, coordinate, and facilitate training programs for admissions counselors. Ensure recruitment activities and operational processes run effectively. Transfer Student Recruitment & Outreach \u2013 20% Lead recruitment efforts for prospective transfer students. Manage an assigned recruitment territory and serve as the primary contact for key Washington community colleges. Build relationships with students, families, advisors, faculty, and administrators. Plan and participate in recruitment events, presentations, and outreach activities. Enrollment Strategy & Stakeholder Collaboration \u2013 10% Partner with campus stakeholders to develop and execute enrollment strategies. Contribute to achieving undergraduate enrollment goals. Attend and support major admissions events such as preview days and admitted student programs. Use data, relationship-building, and strategic planning to improve recruitment outcomes. \u00a0 Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in a relevant field and\u00a0three\u00a0years\u00a0of professional work experience OR an equivalent combination of education and experience sufficient to successfully perform the essential job functions.\u00a0 Must possess well-developed organizational skills, confidence in speaking publicly to both large and small groups, strong oral and written communication skills and establish and maintain cooperative and effective working relationships with others. A commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and supporting diverse student populations. Knowledge of college admission trends and issues in higher education.\u00a0 Ability and willingness to travel and work evenings and weekends, as necessary. Valid driver's license required.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 Preferred Qualifications Speaks, writes, and reads one of the following languages: Spanish, Vietnamese, Somali, Mandarin, Tagalog, Amharic, or Tigrinya. Interested in utilizing language skills to serve prospective students and families in the college search process.\u00a0\u00a0 Knowledge of Washington Community College systems and transfer degrees. Knowledge of Microsoft Office products and federal and state privacy laws.\u00a0\u00a0 Knowledge of financial aid trends and issues.\u00a0\u00a0 Experience using an\u00a0ERP\u00a0and\u00a0CRM, specifically Ellucian Colleague. system,\u00a0Technolutions\u00a0Slate CRM and Workday.\u00a0\u00a0 Experience supervising professional or student staff. Experience working with diverse young adult and youth populations.\u00a0\u00a0 Experience working with international students.\u00a0\u00a0 Military Veteran and/or experience with working with U.S. Military Veterans and/or Military Veteran and/or experience with working with U.S. Military Veterans. Compensation at a Glance $73,000 - $74,000 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Assistant-Professor--Psychology_JR100616": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Psychology", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPosition Description Position Description The Department of Psychology at Seattle University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position from candidates with a familiarity and appreciation of Humanistic and/or Existential-Phenomenological Clinical Psychology beginning August,\u00a0 2027. The Master of Arts in Psychology (MAP) program seeks candidates with experience in clinical teaching, mentorship, and practice, and a demonstrated commitment to work with diverse and/or underserved communities. Candidates should possess knowledge and skills in critical psychology and humanistic theory and practice. Ideally, the candidate has research or scholarly interests in multicultural psychology and therapy in diverse social contexts and/or community-based engagement and programming in mental health care .\u00a0 The responsibilities of the position will include teaching in Seattle University\u2019s clinically focused graduate psychology program and some teaching in its undergraduate psychology program. The new faculty member will be expected to advance a program of ongoing research and scholarship. They will provide service to the graduate program, department, college, and university.\u00a0\u00a0They will assist with the graduate program\u2019s admissions process, with student advising and mentorship, and curriculum development and assessment. The candidate will also have the opportunity to develop classes and teach in their areas of interest and specialization. The Master of Arts in Psychology (MAP) program is grounded in phenomenological and human sciences approaches to clinical training. The broader Psychology Department embraces a variety of theoretical perspectives, including human science, natural science, and social scientific approaches to understanding human experience.\u00a0\u00a0We strongly value dialogue, collegiality, and collaboration across these perspectives, as well as across disciplines of the social sciences and humanities. Qualifications A doctoral degree in Psychology by August 2027. The Department of Psychology is committed to anti-racism through fostering inclusive and equitable practices, honoring lived experiences of community members we seek to work with, and promoting diversity in our curriculum and pedagogical choices.\u00a0 We continue our ongoing work to advance goals of equity and inclusion, guided by our Lift SU, our University inclusive excellence action plan ( https://www.seattleu.edu/office-of-diversity-and-inclusion/lift-su-our-action-plan/ ). We encourage applicants to apply whose work, lived experience, and/or interests align with these commitments. Application Instructions Applicants should submit applications online at www.seattleu.edu/careers, including a cover letter, CV, statement of teaching philosophy, statement of research, and statement describing how you understand the role of humanistic/human science traditions in clinical training. Please also include the names and contact information of three references (letters may be solicited upon submission of application). Application review will begin November 15, 2026. Open until filled. In your cover letter explain how you understand, embody, and would contribute to each of the following at Seattle University. Please dedicate one paragraph to each prompt: Our 5-year Strategic Plan, Reigniting Our Strategic Directions; Our Mission as a Jesuit and Catholic University; Our commitment to DEI and Inclusive Academic Excellence; Our focus on pedagogy, scholarship, service, and institution building; and Our faculty members\u2019 role as Teacher-Scholars. Founded in 1891, Seattle University is a Jesuit Catholic university located on a beautiful campus of more than 50 acres in the dynamic heart of Seattle. Our diverse and driven population is made up of more than 7,200 students enrolled in undergraduate and graduate programs within eight schools and colleges. Seattle University is an equal opportunity employer. In support of its pursuit of academic and scholarly excellence, Seattle University is committed to creating a diverse community of students, faculty and staff that is dedicated to the fundamental principles of equal opportunity and treatment in education and employment regardless of age, color, disability, gender identity, national origin, political ideology, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. The university encourages applications from, and nominations of, individuals who will further enrich the diversity of our educational community. Compensation at a Glance: $74,000 - $77,500 Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. \u202fThe salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. \u202fSeattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. \u202fChoose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs.\u202f Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. \u202fPlan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% non elective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. \u202fYou may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. \u202fEnjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, and paid sick leave.\u202f For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Temporary-Redhawk-Ambassador---Athletics_JR100570-1": {"title": "Temporary Redhawk Ambassador - Athletics", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nVariable time\n\nMonths Per Year: 6 and 8 hours per week Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Redhawk Ambassador serves as the enthusiastic face of Seattle University Athletics, creating exceptional guest experiences at athletic events. This part-time, event-based role combines guest services with light security duties, perfect for outgoing community members who bleed Redhawk red and want to be part of something bigger. Position Description: Key Responsibilities Welcome and assist guests with genuine enthusiasm and school spirit Provide directions, information, and support throughout events Monitor crowd behavior and enforce venue policies in a friendly manner Engage fans to enhance their game day experience and build lasting connections Coordinate with event staff and contracted security as needed Represent Seattle University with pride and professionalism as part of our ambassador community \u00a0 Qualifications Outgoing personality with excellent people skills and life experience Genuine passion for Seattle University and Redhawk athletics Ability to stay calm and professional in busy environments Strong communication and problem-solving abilities Physical ability to stand and walk for extended periods Availability for evenings and weekends \u2013 3-4 hours/event, 2-6 events/month Undergraduate students will NOT be considered for this position. Graduate students may be considered but will be paid at the campus student employment rate if hired. \u00a0 Application Requirements Resume highlighting relevant experience Brief statement on what Seattle University means to you and why you'd be perfect for this role Prior connection to Seattle University (alumni, family, community ties) is a plus \u2013 we love our extended Redhawk family! \u00a0 Additional Details Comprehensive training provided before starting Part-time, event-based schedule Hired on a rolling basis Free admission to all Seattle University athletic events Compensation at a Glance $22.50 per hour Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Career-Engagement-Coordinator_JR100606": {"title": "Career Engagement Coordinator", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nMonths Per Year: 12 Work Model: Onsite Seattle University will be one of the most innovative and progressive Jesuit and Catholic universities in the world, educating with excellence at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. We embrace an intersectional framework for defining diversity in its broadest sense, including differences in gender, gender identity, race, ethnicity, generational history, culture, socioeconomic class, religion, sexual orientation, national origin, citizenship status, political perspectives, geographic origin and physical ability. Seattle University strives to be a welcome, open and safe campus climate for all who learn, live and work at Seattle University. Seattle University's Core Competencies Effective Team Collaboration: Works harmoniously with others towards a common goal, leveraging individual strengths to achieve shared objectives. Emotionally Intelligent Communication: Engages in a skillful exchange of information, thoughts, and feelings, verbally and non-verbally. Ethics and Integrity: Demonstrates an unwavering commitment to honesty, professionalism, and alignment with our Jesuit mission in all actions and decisions. Flexibility and Innovation: Embraces change, approaching problems with curiosity adaptability, and is committed to making a positive impact. Mindful Inclusion: Fosters mindful inclusion by consciously creating a workplace that celebrates differences, embraces cultural humility, and empowers uniqueness. Outcomes-Driven Orientation: Sets clear objectives, pursues excellence, and delivers measurable outcomes while upholding Jesuit Catholic values. Job Description Summary The Career Engagement Coordinator supports the delivery, coordination, and promotion of Career Engagement programs and services for Seattle University students and recent alumni. Serving as a generalist and cross-functional member of the Career Engagement team, this position provides front-line career support to students while coordinating programs, communications, resources, and team initiatives. The Coordinator helps ensure that Career Engagement programs are well organized, effectively promoted, accessible to students, and consistently delivered across the university. The position works collaboratively with Career Education, Employer Engagement, and Career Engagement leadership to support the implementation of strategic initiatives while providing responsive day-to-day support for students and campus partners. Position Description: Career Coaching & Student Support \u2014 35% Provide individual, drop-in, and group career support for students across academic disciplines in ways that are engaging, culturally conscious, and effective. Assist students with resumes, cover letters, interviewing, networking, internship and job searches, career exploration, and use of Career Engagement resources. Help students identify appropriate Career Engagement programs, employer opportunities, experiential learning resources, and campus services. Support student intake and referrals to specialized Career Engagement staff when appropriate. Maintain accurate student engagement data and appointment records. Help ensure a welcoming, accessible, and student-centered experience across Career Engagement services. Program & Team Coordination \u2014 35% Provide coordination and implementation support for Career Engagement programs, workshops, events, and strategic initiatives. Assist with scheduling, logistics, registration, materials, communications, and follow-up for programs and events. Support initiatives such as career fairs, employer programs, networking events, mentorship, Career Champions, workshops, and other student engagement activities. Maintain program calendars, project timelines, participation information, and other shared team resources. Coordinate across Career Education and Employer Engagement to help ensure programs and communications are aligned. Participate in and contribute to Career Engagement team meetings, professional development, and division-wide initiatives. Provide administrative and project support for new Career Engagement initiatives as they are developed. Provide additional program and operational support during high-volume periods. Other duties as assigned. Communications, Marketing & Student Outreach \u2014 15% Coordinate student-facing communications promoting Career Engagement programs, services, events, resources, and opportunities. Draft and distribute newsletters, email campaigns, event promotions, digital announcements, and other student communications. Support Career Engagement social media and digital outreach in collaboration with university communications partners. Maintain a communications and promotional calendar for Career Engagement. Develop and update promotional materials, presentations, templates, and other communication resources. Work with Career Engagement staff to identify programs and opportunities requiring targeted student outreach. Help ensure consistent messaging and branding across Career Engagement communications. Identify opportunities to increase student awareness and engagement with Career Engagement services. Career Technology, Digital Resources & Data \u2014 15% Help students navigate Career Engagement technology and digital career resources like Handshake, ConnectSU, Redhawk Hub, and more. Support collection and maintenance of program participation, student engagement, and communications data. Support maintenance and updating of student-facing resources, web content, and career information. Assist with implementation and promotion of new career technologies and AI-enabled career resources. Identify recurring student questions and resource gaps and share insights with the team. Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. One to two years of experience in student services, higher education, career services, recruiting, communications/marketing, event or program coordination, customer service, or a related area. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to develop clear and engaging student-facing communications. Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to coordinate multiple projects, programs, events, or deadlines. Demonstrated ability to provide responsive, high-quality service and work effectively with a variety of individuals and stakeholders. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team while independently managing assigned responsibilities. Comfort learning and using digital platforms, communication tools, databases, and other technology. Demonstrated commitment to working effectively with students and communities from diverse backgrounds. Commitment to Seattle University\u2019s Jesuit mission and values. Preferred Qualifications Experience working with college students or recent graduates. Experience in career services, academic advising, student affairs, recruiting, human resources, or another student-facing environment. Experience coordinating events, workshops, educational programs, or student engagement activities. Experience developing email campaigns, newsletters, social media content, digital communications, or other marketing materials. Familiarity with career development topics such as resumes, interviewing, networking, career exploration, and internship/job searching. Experience using career management, CRM, email marketing, website/content management, or event-management platforms. Experience supporting employer, alumni, faculty, or community partner engagement. Experience collecting, maintaining, or reporting program participation and engagement data. Interest in emerging career-development technologies, including AI-enabled career tools. Compensation at a Glance $47,569.60 - $59,841.60 Application Instructions Please attach a cover letter with your resume when applying. Job postings are open until filled, unless otherwise specified. Seattle University has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, and not based on a candidate\u2019s gender or any other protected status. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Seattle University provides generous leave, health plans, and retirement contributions that add to your total compensation package. Benefits at a Glance Consistent with its fundamental Jesuit values, Seattle University offers a wide range of benefits designed to care for the whole person. Choose from three different medical plans, a dental, and vision insurance programs. Protect your income with life, short & long-term disability coverage. Plan for your future with up to a 10% employer contribution for retirement benefits, comprised of a 5% nonelective employer contribution and an additional dollar-for-dollar match of your voluntary contributions up to a maximum of 5%. You may also take advantage of 100% paid tuition benefits for the employee and dependents, a subsidized transportation benefit, a wellness program with free access to an onsite fitness facility, and a wide variety of campus events. Enjoy a generous holiday schedule, including a paid Holiday break closure in December, vacation and paid sick leave, and paid community service leave. For more information explore the Benefits website at: https://www.seattleu.edu/hr/benefits/ Positions designated as hybrid eligible may offer the opportunity for a hybrid work schedule after successful completion of an introductory period of 3\u20136 months. In these cases, the role typically begins fully on campus to support onboarding and training, with the potential to transition to a hybrid arrangement thereafter. Positions designated as on-site are not eligible for hybrid work and are expected to remain fully on campus. All flexible work arrangements are subject to periodic review and may be modified or discontinued at any time at the university\u2019s discretion."}}, "rows": {"https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Chief-of-Staff-to-the-President_JR100363": {"school_id": 1519, "posting_url": "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Chief-of-Staff-to-the-President_JR100363", "job_title_in_post": "Chief of Staff to the President", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Staff to the President", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-09-04"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Digital-Operations---Customer-Experience-Coordinator_JR100082": {"school_id": 1519, "posting_url": "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Digital-Operations---Customer-Experience-Coordinator_JR100082", "job_title_in_post": "Digital Operations & Customer Experience Coordinator", "position_type": "Non-academic", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-09-02"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Kinesiology-Program-Manager_JR100154": {"school_id": 1519, "posting_url": "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/Seattle-University/Kinesiology-Program-Manager_JR100154", "job_title_in_post": "Kinesiology Program Manager", "position_type": "Non-academic", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-08-27"}, "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Instructor--Biology_JR100318-1": {"school_id": 1519, "posting_url": "https://seattleu.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/SeattleUJobs/job/First-Hill/Instructor--Biology_JR100318-1", "job_title_in_post": "Instructor, Biology", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": 